{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MuR17QnfzX5N4a8KYsQ5L3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"real_data_dif_mmlu\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Apply MH-DIF analysis to METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 items). Temporal cohort (2023 vs 2024-H1), ability-quintile cohort, within-family confound control, domain-specificity analysis, Li et al. proxy validation, SIBTEST non-uniform DIF check.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"temporal_ets_c_pct\": 31.3,\n7\t      \"temporal_ets_b_pct\": 20.1,\n8\t      \"temporal_ets_a_pct\": 48.6,\n9\t      \"temporal_n_ref\": 1253,\n10\t      \"temporal_n_foc\": 656,\n11\t      \"temporal_fpr_li_clean\": 0.318,\n12\t      \"temporal_enrichment\": 0.96,\n13\t      \"ability_ets_c_pct\": 2.9,\n14\t      \"ability_enrichment\": 1.19,\n15\t      \"temporal_ability_jaccard\": 0.014,\n16\t      \"within_family_llama2v3_c_pct\": 61.3,\n17\t      \"within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct\": 38.7,\n18\t      \"domain_spearman_rho\": 0.307,\n19\t      \"domain_spearman_p\": 0.02,\n20\t      \"sanity_random_split_c_pct\": 0.18,\n21\t      \"sibtest_nonuniform_additional_pct\": 11.2,\n22\t      \"sibtest_nonuniform_items\": 1406,\n23\t      \"mh_sibtest_uniform_jaccard\": 0.71,\n24\t      \"sibtest_total_flagged_pct\": 42.5,\n25\t      \"sibtest_crossing_icc_pct\": 7.8,\n26\t      \"sibtest_amplifying_pct\": 3.4,\n27\t      \"negative_discrimination_items\": 1927,\n28\t      \"negative_discrimination_pct\": 15.4,\n29\t      \"sensitivity_with_neg_disc_removed_c_pct\": 29.8\n30\t    },\n31\t    \"tables\": [\n32\t      {\n33\t        \"name\": \"Table 1: DIF prevalence across cohort definitions and item filtering\",\n34\t        \"description\": \"4-way sensitivity matrix: {temporal, ability} × {with, without negative-discrimination items}. Shows temporal C%=31.3% (29.8% without neg-disc items), ability C%=2.9%. Includes ETS A/B/C breakdown and SIBTEST non-uniform counts for each cell.\\n\"\n35\t      },\n36\t      {\n37\t        \"name\": \"Table 2: Within-family DIF analysis\",\n38\t        \"description\": \"Family pairs (Llama-2 vs Llama-3, Mistral vs Mixtral) with C%, item overlap with temporal DIF, and interpretation (capability gain vs contamination differential).\\n\"\n39\t      },\n40\t      {\n41\t        \"name\": \"Table S1: SIBTEST concordance with MH\",\n42\t        \"description\": \"Cross-tabulation of MH-flagged vs SIBTEST-flagged items. Uniform-only (MH∩SIBTEST): 2,789. MH-only: 1,129. SIBTEST non-uniform only: 1,406. Neither: 7,184. Jaccard=0.71.\\n\"\n43\t      }\n44\t    ],\n45\t    \"figures\": [],\n46\t    \"analysis\": \"The core finding is robust: ~31% of MMLU items function differently across model generations, and this is not a statistical artifact (sanity check: 0.18%), not driven by ability differences (ability cohort: 2.9%), and predominantly item-specific (domain improvement explains ≤7.6%). The SIBTEST extension reveals MH underestimates total DIF by ~11pp, requiring a new paragraph in §3 acknowledging non-uniform DIF as an MH limitation. The framing becomes: \\\"MH captures the dominant uniform DIF signal (31.3% of items); SIBTEST concordance (Jaccard=0.71) confirms the additional ~11% non-uniform DIF items exist but do not qualitatively change the instability finding — if anything, they strengthen it.\\\" This is honest, preemptive, and turns a potential weakness into evidence of thoroughness.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier the core instability finding (31.3% ETS C) holds exactly — this is the anchor result around which the paper is built. Random-split sanity check (0.18%) and ability-cohort orthogonality (Jaccard=0.014) confirm the signal is temporal, not statistical artifact. However, SIBTEST reveals 11.2% additional items with significant non-uniform DIF (crossing ICCs where the item favors one cohort at low ability but the other at high ability). MH, by design, only detects uniform DIF (constant direction across ability levels). The MH-SIBTEST Jaccard of 0.71 shows MH captures the dominant signal but misses ~1,400 items with crossing patterns. This is expected for a 60-year-old method applied to a heterogeneous LLM population, but requires honest reporting: total temporal instability is ~42.5%, not 31.3%. The uniform:non-uniform ratio (3:1) supports framing MH as capturing the dominant mode. Crossing ICCs (7.8%) are particularly interesting — these items favor older models at low ability but newer models at high ability, potentially reflecting genuine capability structure changes. Domain correlation (ρ=0.307) remains modest; the item-level decomposition from plan_019 (≤2.4pp/7.6% explained) is the stronger defense. Within-family analysis shows Llama-2→3 transition has extreme DIF (61.3% C), consistent with a major architecture+data shift, while Mistral→Mixtral is lower (38.7%), reflecting the mixture-of-experts change being less disruptive to item functioning.\\n\"\n47\t  },\n48\t  {\n49\t    \"name\": \"rl_robustness_validation\",\n50\t    \"description\": \"Validate DIF robustness to RL training. SFT baseline, alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO, N=477), and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO, N=13) cohort comparisons. Traditional detector degradation analysis. Power analysis for partial exploitation rates.\\n\",\n51\t    \"results\": {\n52\t      \"sft_cohort_n\": 312,\n53\t      \"alignment_rl_cohort_n\": 477,\n54\t      \"reasoning_rl_models_n\": 13,\n55\t      \"reasoning_rl_models_list\": \"DeepSeek-R1-7B, DeepSeek-R1-32B, Qwen2.5-7B-RL, Qwen2.5-14B-RL, Qwen2.5-32B-RL, Llama-3.1-8B-GRPO, Llama-3.1-70B-GRPO, Phi-3-mini-GRPO, Mistral-7B-PPO, Yi-1.5-9B-RL, Gemma-2-9B-RL, InternLM2-7B-RL, MAP-Neo-7B-RL\",\n56\t      \"dif_flag_retention_sft_vs_alignrl_jaccard\": 0.83,\n57\t      \"dif_flag_retention_sft_vs_reasonrl_jaccard\": 0.69,\n58\t      \"dif_flag_retention_alignrl_vs_reasonrl_jaccard\": 0.72,\n59\t      \"constat_auroc_sft\": 0.72,\n60\t      \"constat_auroc_alignrl\": 0.68,\n61\t      \"constat_auroc_reasonrl\": 0.51,\n62\t      \"constat_auroc_reasonrl_ci\": [\n63\t        0.38,\n64\t        0.64\n65\t      ],\n66\t      \"mink_auroc_sft\": 0.69,\n67\t      \"mink_auroc_alignrl\": 0.64,\n68\t      \"mink_auroc_reasonrl\": 0.48,\n69\t      \"mink_auroc_reasonrl_ci\": [\n70\t        0.34,\n71\t        0.62\n72\t      ],\n73\t      \"dif_cohort_c_pct_with_rl_added\": 30.8,\n74\t      \"dif_cohort_c_pct_without_rl\": 31.3,\n75\t      \"delong_p_constat_sft_vs_reasonrl\": 0.04,\n76\t      \"delong_p_mink_sft_vs_reasonrl\": 0.03,\n77\t      \"tost_equivalence_dif_retention_p\": 0.08,\n78\t      \"power_analysis_exploitation_p04_tpr\": 0.31,\n79\t      \"power_analysis_exploitation_p06_tpr\": 0.47,\n80\t      \"power_analysis_exploitation_p07_tpr\": 0.58,\n81\t      \"power_analysis_exploitation_p10_tpr\": 0.72,\n82\t      \"detection_boundary_80pct_power\": \"p≈0.85, δ≈1.7\",\n83\t      \"bootstrap_ci_retention_jaccard_reasonrl\": [\n84\t        0.54,\n85\t        0.81\n86\t      ]\n87\t    },\n88\t    \"tables\": [\n89\t      {\n90\t        \"name\": \"Table S2: DIF retention across training paradigms\",\n91\t        \"description\": \"Rows: SFT, Alignment-RL, Reasoning-RL. Columns: N, cohort C%, Jaccard vs SFT baseline, bootstrap 95% CI. Shows stability for alignment-RL, uncertainty for reasoning-RL.\\n\"\n92\t      },\n93\t      {\n94\t        \"name\": \"Table S3: Traditional detector degradation on RL models\",\n95\t        \"description\": \"ConStat and Min-K% AUROC by training paradigm (SFT/Align-RL/Reason-RL), with DeLong p-values and 95% CIs. Shows significant degradation on reasoning-RL.\\n\"\n96\t      }\n97\t    ],\n98\t    \"figures\": [],\n99\t    \"analysis\": \"The two-level validation design (group-level DIF vs model-level traditional) is theoretically clean but empirically asymmetric: the alignment-RL arm has adequate power (N=477) while the reasoning-RL arm (N=13) is severely underpowered. In median tier, this asymmetry is exactly what a careful reviewer would flag — the claim that DIF is RL-robust rests on the less interesting arm (DPO doesn't use PPO clipping anyway) while the critical arm (GRPO/PPO) is inconclusive. The power analysis honestly characterizes the detection boundary but doesn't resolve the core question. Paper strategy: present alignment-RL result in supplementary as \\\"confirmed robustness to offline preference optimization,\\\" acknowledge reasoning-RL as the key open question in §5 limitations, and frame the power analysis as roadmap for future validation. Do NOT claim RL robustness as a contribution.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, the RL robustness story is partially supported but critically underpowered for the reasoning-RL arm. Alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO) cohort shows good DIF flag retention (Jaccard=0.83 vs SFT) — expected because DPO doesn't use PPO clipping and doesn't produce the NLL gap contraction that conceals contamination. Adding 477 alignment-RL models to the focal cohort barely changes C% (31.3→30.8%), confirming DIF is robust to this RL variant. The reasoning-RL comparison is the problem: N=13 models yields Jaccard=0.69 with a bootstrap CI of [0.54, 0.81] — too wide to distinguish \\\"partial degradation\\\" from \\\"no degradation\\\" or \\\"serious degradation.\\\" TOST equivalence test fails (p=0.08), meaning we cannot formally claim DIF retention is equivalent across RL types. Traditional detectors DO degrade on reasoning-RL (ConStat AUROC 0.72→0.51, Min-K% 0.69→0.48), and DeLong tests confirm these drops are significant (p<0.05) — but this comparison is apples-to-oranges (group-level DIF vs model-level detectors). Power analysis shows DIF's detection boundary at p≈0.85 exploitation rate for 80% power — reasonable for full memorization but weak for partial exploitation. The N=13 reasoning-RL limitation is fundamental: more open-weight GRPO/PPO models simply don't exist yet in METABENCH. This experiment provides theoretical motivation but not empirical proof. Demoted to limitations section with honest framing: \\\"Alignment-RL robustness confirmed (N=477); reasoning-RL robustness remains an open question due to small sample (N=13). As reasoning-RL models proliferate, this validation becomes feasible.\\\"\\n\"\n100\t  },\n101\t  {\n102\t    \"name\": \"constat_perturbation_eval\",\n103\t    \"description\": \"Independent ConStat-style perturbation testing on MMLU. Generate semantically equivalent reformulations for 218 items, compare original vs reformulated performance, apply FDR-controlled detection, characterize power by training paradigm.\\n\",\n104\t    \"results\": {\n105\t      \"n_items_paraphrased\": 218,\n106\t      \"paraphrase_method\": \"GPT-4 with construct-preservation constraints\",\n107\t      \"paraphrase_semantic_similarity_mean\": 0.891,\n108\t      \"paraphrase_semantic_similarity_std\": 0.062,\n109\t      \"paraphrase_quality_human_eval_accuracy\": 0.76,\n110\t      \"paraphrase_quality_human_eval_n_raters\": 3,\n111\t      \"constat_detection_rate_overall\": 0.28,\n112\t      \"constat_detection_rate_non_rl\": 0.37,\n113\t      \"constat_detection_rate_alignment_rl\": 0.31,\n114\t      \"constat_detection_rate_reasoning_rl\": 0.11,\n115\t      \"constat_fdr_controlled\": 0.08,\n116\t      \"constat_fpr\": 0.06,\n117\t      \"constat_fpr_ci\": [\n118\t        0.03,\n119\t        0.1\n120\t      ],\n121\t      \"effect_size_mean_overall\": 0.64,\n122\t      \"effect_size_mean_non_rl\": 0.82,\n123\t      \"effect_size_mean_alignment_rl\": 0.71,\n124\t      \"effect_size_mean_reasoning_rl\": 0.29,\n125\t      \"dif_constat_overlap_jaccard\": 0.23,\n126\t      \"dif_constat_union_coverage\": 0.41,\n127\t      \"items_constat_only\": 87,\n128\t      \"items_dif_only\": 312,\n129\t      \"items_both\": 64\n130\t    },\n131\t    \"tables\": [\n132\t      {\n133\t        \"name\": \"Table S4: ConStat detection power by training paradigm\",\n134\t        \"description\": \"Rows: Non-RL, Alignment-RL, Reasoning-RL, Overall. Columns: N, detection rate, mean effect size, FDR. Shows clear degradation gradient from non-RL to reasoning-RL.\\n\"\n135\t      }\n136\t    ],\n137\t    \"figures\": [],\n138\t    \"analysis\": \"ConStat replication confirms the literature's findings on non-RL models but adds limited value beyond what's already published. The low DIF-ConStat overlap (Jaccard=0.23) is the most interesting finding — it demonstrates that DIF and perturbation approaches target fundamentally different contamination modes. This supports the paper's positioning of DIF as a novel lens rather than a competing method. However, 218 items is a small subset of MMLU's 12,508, and paraphrase quality concerns limit confidence. In median tier, this is appendix material that serves primarily to contextualize DIF's contribution relative to existing methods.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, ConStat provides a useful but limited baseline comparison. The 37% detection rate on non-RL models shows perturbation sensitivity is a real signal, but the 11% rate on reasoning-RL models confirms the theoretical prediction that models trained with online RL develop robustness to surface-level reformulations. The DIF-ConStat Jaccard of 0.23 is low, indicating the two methods detect largely different item sets — DIF catches items with differential difficulty across cohorts (conditioning on ability), while ConStat catches items where models exploit surface features (verbatim memorization). This low overlap is actually informative: it shows the two approaches target different contamination mechanisms. However, paraphrase quality (human eval 0.76) means ~24% of reformulations may introduce construct-irrelevant variance — a methodological weakness a reviewer could exploit. In median tier, ConStat results go to appendix as baseline comparison, supporting the narrative that \\\"perturbation methods and DIF capture orthogonal contamination dimensions, but perturbation methods degrade under RL.\\\" The low Jaccard is cited in §2 Related Work to position DIF as complementary, not competing.\\n\"\n139\t  },\n140\t  {\n141\t    \"name\": \"dvd_generation_variance_eval\",\n142\t    \"description\": \"DVD generation variance analysis on MMLU. Temperature-sampled responses (50 per item) from 12 open-weight models. Bimodal distribution detection and variance-based contamination scoring.\\n\",\n143\t    \"results\": {\n144\t      \"n_models_tested\": 12,\n145\t      \"models_list\": \"Llama-2-7B, Llama-2-13B, Llama-3-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.1, Mistral-7B-v0.3, Phi-3-mini, Qwen-1.5-7B, Qwen-2-7B, Yi-1.5-9B, Gemma-7B, Gemma-2-9B, InternLM2-7B\",\n146\t      \"n_items_sampled\": 200,\n147\t      \"n_generations_per_item\": 50,\n148\t      \"temperature\": 0.8,\n149\t      \"bimodal_detection_rate\": 0.14,\n150\t      \"bimodal_detection_ci\": [\n151\t        0.09,\n152\t        0.2\n153\t      ],\n154\t      \"variance_score_auroc\": 0.61,\n155\t      \"variance_score_auroc_ci\": [\n156\t        0.52,\n157\t        0.7\n158\t      ],\n159\t      \"verbatim_contamination_sensitivity\": 0.43,\n160\t      \"variant_contamination_sensitivity\": 0.18,\n161\t      \"false_positive_rate\": 0.12,\n162\t      \"dif_dvd_overlap_jaccard\": 0.15,\n163\t      \"compute_cost_gpu_hours\": 62,\n164\t      \"inference_failures_pct\": 3.2,\n165\t      \"mcq_format_compliance_rate\": 0.91\n166\t    },\n167\t    \"tables\": [\n168\t      {\n169\t        \"name\": \"Table S5: DVD performance by model and contamination type\",\n170\t        \"description\": \"12 models × {verbatim, variant, clean} items. Variance scores, bimodal detection rates, and AUROC per model. Shows model-level heterogeneity (some models more amenable to DVD than others, no clear pattern).\\n\"\n171\t      }\n172\t    ],\n173\t    \"figures\": [],\n174\t    \"analysis\": \"DVD's failure on MCQ benchmarks is an informative negative result. The method was designed for open-ended generation where contaminated models produce stereotyped outputs with low variance. MCQ format constrains all models to categorical outputs (A/B/C/D), compressing the variance signal that DVD relies on. This is a structural limitation, not a tuning issue. In median tier, this is cleanly negative and excluded from further analysis. One paragraph in appendix.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, DVD underperforms expectations. The bimodal distribution signal (14% of items) is real but rare — most contaminated items don't produce the clean bimodal (memorized vs non-memorized generation) pattern that DVD theory predicts, likely because MCQ format constrains generation variance (models commit to A/B/C/D, producing categorical rather than continuous distributions). AUROC of 0.61 with CI [0.52, 0.70] is barely above chance at the lower bound. Variant contamination sensitivity (0.18) is particularly disappointing — DVD's theoretical advantage over perturbation methods is detecting non-verbatim memorization, but this fails to materialize. The 62 GPU-hour cost for 12 models × 200 items × 50 generations is disproportionate to the signal strength. Format compliance issues (9% of generations don't produce valid MCQ answers) add noise. The DIF-DVD Jaccard of 0.15 shows almost no overlap, suggesting DVD and DIF detect unrelated item properties. In median tier, DVD is a clear negative result: the method doesn't port well from open-ended generation (its original domain) to MCQ benchmarks. Excluded from ensemble (plan_003c), brief appendix mention as negative finding.\\n\"\n175\t  },\n176\t  {\n177\t    \"name\": \"ensemble_fusion\",\n178\t    \"description\": \"Fuse DIF + ConStat into 2-signal ensemble (DVD excluded due to near-chance performance). Calibrate thresholds, evaluate majority voting and weighted fusion, cross-validate.\\n\",\n179\t    \"results\": {\n180\t      \"ensemble_signals\": 2,\n181\t      \"ensemble_auroc_majority\": 0.73,\n182\t      \"ensemble_auroc_weighted\": 0.77,\n183\t      \"ensemble_auroc_weighted_ci\": [\n184\t        0.7,\n185\t        0.84\n186\t      ],\n187\t      \"dif_standalone_auroc\": 0.74,\n188\t      \"constat_standalone_auroc\": 0.66,\n189\t      \"improvement_over_best_single\": 0.03,\n190\t      \"improvement_ci\": [\n191\t        -0.02,\n192\t        0.08\n193\t      ],\n194\t      \"ensemble_fpr\": 0.09,\n195\t      \"ensemble_tpr_delta_ge_06\": 0.52,\n196\t      \"ensemble_tpr_delta_ge_14\": 0.74,\n197\t      \"fusion_weight_dif\": 0.62,\n198\t      \"fusion_weight_constat\": 0.38,\n199\t      \"cross_validation_5fold_mean_auroc\": 0.75,\n200\t      \"cross_validation_5fold_std\": 0.04,\n201\t      \"uniform_weight_auroc\": 0.74\n202\t    },\n203\t    \"tables\": [\n204\t      {\n205\t        \"name\": \"Table S6: Ensemble vs standalone detection performance\",\n206\t        \"description\": \"Comparison of DIF-only, ConStat-only, Majority-2, Weighted-2, and Uniform-2 configurations. AUROC, TPR at δ≥{0.6, 1.0, 1.4}, FPR, and 5-fold CV variance.\\n\"\n207\t      }\n208\t    ],\n209\t    \"figures\": [],\n210\t    \"analysis\": \"The ensemble was the original Claim 2 pillar — a multi-signal detection system with broader coverage than any single method. In median tier, this vision collapses: DVD fails on MCQ, ConStat adds marginal signal, and the 2-method ensemble doesn't statistically outperform DIF alone. This is actually a clean narrative outcome — it supports the paper's pivot from \\\"detection system\\\" to \\\"diagnostic lens.\\\" DIF's value isn't as one component of a detection pipeline but as a psychometric characterization tool. The negative ensemble result reinforces the framing change. Brief appendix mention explaining why ensemble was attempted and why it doesn't add value.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, the ensemble degrades to a 2-signal combination after DVD exclusion. The weighted fusion AUROC (0.77) marginally exceeds DIF standalone (0.74) by +0.03, but the improvement CI [−0.02, 0.08] includes zero — not statistically significant. Majority voting (0.73) actually underperforms DIF alone because ConStat's lower precision drags down the consensus. Fusion weights (DIF 0.62, ConStat 0.38) heavily favor DIF, and the uniform-weight baseline (0.74) matches DIF standalone — confirming ConStat adds negligible complementary signal. Cross-validation variance (std=0.04) suggests the +0.03 improvement is within noise. The theoretical coverage argument (DIF for δ≥1.4, ConStat for non-RL δ≥0.6) partially holds, but the practical overlap in detectable items is too low (Jaccard=0.23) for fusion to exploit synergies — the methods disagree more than they complement. In median tier, this is a clear negative: ensemble doesn't justify added complexity. The paper's story is better served by presenting DIF as a standalone method with distinct properties rather than positioning it as part of a detection ensemble. Dropped from main body entirely.\\n\"\n211\t  },\n212\t  {\n213\t    \"name\": \"cross_benchmark_gsm8k\",\n214\t    \"description\": \"Replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models from METABENCH). GSM1K contamination gap validation. Semi-synthetic injection on GSM8K. Item difficulty confound analysis.\\n\",\n215\t    \"results\": {\n216\t      \"step1_gsm8k_temporal_dif\": {\n217\t        \"n_items\": 1319,\n218\t        \"n_models_total\": 6702,\n219\t        \"n_ref_2023\": 1017,\n220\t        \"n_foc_2024h1\": 804,\n221\t        \"standard_c_pct\": 18.9,\n222\t        \"external_c_pct\": 47.4,\n223\t        \"sanity_c_pct\": 0.3,\n224\t        \"standard_external_spearman\": 0.926\n225\t      },\n226\t      \"step2_semi_synthetic\": {\n227\t        \"standard_delta_tpr_p05\": 0.612,\n228\t        \"standard_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.818,\n229\t        \"external_delta_tpr_p05\": 0.389,\n230\t        \"external_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.517,\n231\t        \"cross_subject_delta_fpr\": 0.0\n232\t      },\n233\t      \"step3_gsm1k_correlation\": {\n234\t        \"n_models_matched\": 33,\n235\t        \"n_dif_c_items\": 249,\n236\t        \"pearson_r\": -0.478,\n237\t        \"pearson_p\": 0.005,\n238\t        \"pearson_bca_ci\": [\n239\t          -0.714,\n240\t          -0.155\n241\t        ],\n242\t        \"spearman_rho\": -0.403,\n243\t        \"spearman_p\": 0.02,\n244\t        \"spearman_bca_ci\": [\n245\t          -0.687,\n246\t          -0.031\n247\t        ],\n248\t        \"partial_r_controlling_accuracy\": -0.385,\n249\t        \"partial_p_accuracy\": 0.027,\n250\t        \"partial_r_controlling_accuracy_and_difficulty\": -0.289,\n251\t        \"partial_p_accuracy_and_difficulty\": 0.108,\n252\t        \"partial_r_bca_ci_acc_diff\": [\n253\t          -0.551,\n254\t          0.012\n255\t        ],\n256\t        \"partial_r_controlling_acc_diff_modelsize\": -0.251,\n257\t        \"partial_p_acc_diff_modelsize\": 0.172,\n258\t        \"partial_r_bca_ci_acc_diff_size\": [\n259\t          -0.513,\n260\t          0.048\n261\t        ],\n262\t        \"item_difficulty_dif_correlation\": -0.341,\n263\t        \"item_difficulty_dif_p\": 0.003,\n264\t        \"dif_c_mean_difficulty\": 0.412,\n265\t        \"non_dif_mean_difficulty\": 0.587,\n266\t        \"difficulty_confound_variance_explained\": 0.116,\n267\t        \"permutation_p_raw\": 0.014,\n268\t        \"permutation_p_partial_acc_diff\": 0.098,\n269\t        \"cook_d_max_model\": \"Deepseek-math-7b-rl\",\n270\t        \"cook_d_max_value\": 0.287,\n271\t        \"leave_one_out_r_range\": [\n272\t          -0.537,\n273\t          -0.374\n274\t        ],\n275\t        \"dif_c_favor_2024\": 78,\n276\t        \"dif_c_favor_2023\": 171\n277\t      },\n278\t      \"step3_median_tier_analysis\": {\n279\t        \"degrees_of_freedom_3controls\": 28,\n280\t        \"power_at_r025_n33\": 0.21,\n281\t        \"power_at_r035_n33\": 0.38,\n282\t        \"required_n_for_power80_r030\": 84\n283\t      }\n284\t    },\n285\t    \"tables\": [\n286\t      {\n287\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: GSM8K temporal DIF and semi-synthetic validation\",\n288\t        \"description\": \"Steps 1-2 results: C%, ΔTPR dose-response, ΔFPR. Confirms framework portability. Main body.\\n\"\n289\t      },\n290\t      {\n291\t        \"name\": \"Table S7: GSM1K correlation sensitivity analysis\",\n292\t        \"description\": \"Rows: Raw, +accuracy, +accuracy+difficulty, +accuracy+difficulty+size. Columns: partial r, p-value, BCa CI, permutation p. Shows progressive attenuation from −0.478 to −0.251. Supplementary (moved from main results).\\n\"\n293\t      },\n294\t      {\n295\t        \"name\": \"Table S8: Item difficulty confound characterization\",\n296\t        \"description\": \"DIF-C vs non-DIF items: mean difficulty, difficulty distribution, correlation with DIF effect size. Shows DIF-C items are systematically harder.\\n\"\n297\t      }\n298\t    ],\n299\t    \"figures\": [],\n300\t    \"analysis\": \"The GSM1K correlation is the median tier's central vulnerability. The raw finding (r=−0.478) is real but not the whole story. Item difficulty is a plausible mediator OR confounder — DIF items are harder, harder items are more resistant to contamination-based gains, and harder items are more likely to show DIF because they have more variance in response patterns across model generations. This creates an identifiability problem: is the anti-correlation evidence that \\\"DIF captures genuine capability differences\\\" (the causal claim) or simply that \\\"harder items resist shallow memorization\\\" (a confound-driven association)? With N=33 and the difficulty confound, we cannot distinguish these hypotheses. The paper strategy: move GSM1K correlation from §4 main results to supplementary evidence, explicitly presenting the difficulty confound analysis. Downgrade the language from \\\"DIF items resist contamination-based gains\\\" to \\\"suggestive evidence consistent with DIF capturing genuine capability differences, partially confounded by item difficulty (N=33, exploratory).\\\" Claim 4 then rests on sub-findings 1-3 (redundancy, circularity ablation, diagnostic lens), which are self-contained on N=5,227 and don't depend on GSM1K. This is honest and defensible.\\n\\n\\nSteps 1-2 replicate cleanly: GSM8K temporal DIF (C%=18.9%, lower than MMLU's 31.3% due to single-domain homogeneity) and semi-synthetic framework (ΔTPR=+0.818) both confirm portability. Cross-subject matching continues to eliminate FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000). The median-tier weakness concentrates in Step 3: the raw GSM1K correlation (r=−0.478, p=0.005) survives basic scrutiny, but item difficulty is a genuine confound. DIF-C items are systematically harder (mean difficulty 0.412 vs 0.587 for non-DIF items, correlation −0.341, p=0.003), explaining 11.6% of variance in DIF status. Harder items naturally show larger contamination gaps because contamination-based gains concentrate on items models would otherwise get wrong — and harder items have more room for this. Controlling for both accuracy AND item difficulty drops partial r from −0.478 to −0.289 (p=0.108), with BCa CI [−0.551, 0.012] now including zero at the upper bound. Adding model size as a third control further attenuates to r=−0.251 (p=0.172). With N=33 and 3 controls, df≈28, and power to detect r=0.30 is only 0.38 — the test is underpowered to distinguish a real moderate effect from zero. Permutation p for the partial correlation is 0.098 — suggestive but not significant at α=0.05. Leave-one-out analysis shows robustness to individual influential models (range [−0.537, −0.374] for raw r), but this doesn't address the difficulty confound. The point estimate (r≈−0.29) still suggests DIF items resist contamination-based gains, but the evidence is now \\\"suggestive and exploratory\\\" rather than \\\"confirmed.\\\" N=84 would be needed for 80% power at this effect size — achievable if GSM1K coverage expands.\\n\"\n301\t  },\n302\t  {\n303\t    \"name\": \"bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\",\n304\t    \"description\": \"Pilot bifactor IRT model on MMLU for anchor item selection and equated score calibration. Test whether bifactor specification addresses unidimensional IRT misfit. Fixed-parameter calibration of replacement items.\\n\",\n305\t    \"results\": {\n306\t      \"bifactor_convergence_full\": false,\n307\t      \"bifactor_convergence_partial\": \"32/57 subjects converge\",\n308\t      \"converged_subjects_mean_n_items\": 247,\n309\t      \"failed_subjects_mean_n_items\": 84,\n310\t      \"general_factor_variance_explained\": 0.41,\n311\t      \"subject_factor_avg_variance\": 0.13,\n312\t      \"subject_factor_max_variance\": 0.29,\n313\t      \"subject_factor_min_variance\": 0.02,\n314\t      \"rmsea_bifactor_converged\": 0.038,\n315\t      \"rmsea_unidimensional\": 0.041,\n316\t      \"cfi_bifactor_converged\": 0.87,\n317\t      \"cfi_unidimensional\": 0.82,\n318\t      \"cfi_target\": 0.9,\n319\t      \"anchor_items_selected\": 1847,\n320\t      \"anchor_items_dif_free_pct\": 92.3,\n321\t      \"anchor_items_per_subject_mean\": 57.7,\n322\t      \"equated_rho_5pct_replace\": 0.982,\n323\t      \"equated_rho_10pct_replace\": 0.964,\n324\t      \"equated_rho_20pct_replace\": 0.937,\n325\t      \"equated_rho_31pct_replace\": 0.908,\n326\t      \"equated_rho_target\": 0.95,\n327\t      \"target_met_at_pct\": 14,\n328\t      \"calibration_rmse_difficulty\": 0.31,\n329\t      \"calibration_rmse_discrimination\": 0.28,\n330\t      \"computation_hours\": 38\n331\t    },\n332\t    \"tables\": [\n333\t      {\n334\t        \"name\": \"Table S9: Bifactor IRT fit comparison\",\n335\t        \"description\": \"Unidimensional vs bifactor fit indices (RMSEA, CFI, TLI, SRMR) on converged subjects. Shows improvement but still below standard benchmarks.\\n\"\n336\t      },\n337\t      {\n338\t        \"name\": \"Table S10: Equating quality by replacement rate\",\n339\t        \"description\": \"Replacement rate (5/10/14/20/31%) × Spearman ρ, Kendall τ, top-10 overlap, calibration RMSE. Critical threshold highlighted at 14%.\\n\"\n340\t      }\n341\t    ],\n342\t    \"figures\": [],\n343\t    \"analysis\": \"The bifactor pilot confirms that unidimensional IRT misfit on MMLU is structural (57 subjects) and partially addressable but not solvable with bifactor specification. This result serves two purposes in median tier: (1) it honestly demonstrates the IRT limitation and the concrete failure mode (partial convergence, below-target CFI), providing material for the expanded §5 limitations section that a reviewer will demand; (2) it motivates the specific \\\"multidimensional IRT with domain-specific factors\\\" future work recommendation with empirical evidence (bifactor's partial success shows the direction is right, just needs more flexible specification). The equating quality curve provides practical guidance: benchmark developers can safely replace up to ~14% of items while maintaining ρ≥0.95. Appendix material.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, the bifactor model partially addresses IRT misfit but doesn't fully solve it. Full convergence fails because 25/57 MMLU subjects have insufficient items (mean 84) or near-zero subject-specific variance for stable bifactor estimation. The converged subset shows improved fit (CFI 0.82→0.87, RMSEA 0.041→0.038), but CFI remains below the 0.90 benchmark — MMLU's 57-subject structure is too complex for bifactor (which assumes a single general factor plus subject-specific factors; MMLU likely needs a hierarchical structure with domain clusters). Anchor item selection works well within converged subjects: 1,847 items with 92.3% DIF-free status, providing a stable calibration foundation. However, equating quality degrades sharply with replacement rate: ρ=0.982 at 5% but only 0.908 at 31% (the full DIF-C proportion), falling below the 0.95 target at ~14% replacement. Calibration RMSE of 0.31 for difficulty means replacement items are only approximately matched — each replacement introduces ±0.31 logits of difficulty error, which compounds at high replacement rates. In median tier, this is an honest negative pilot: the approach works for small-scale replacement (≤14%) but doesn't scale to the full DIF remediation needed. Demoted to appendix as methodological pilot, with the IRT misfit result feeding into the §5 limitations expansion. The partial convergence is actually useful evidence for the \\\"future multidimensional IRT work\\\" recommendation.\\n\"\n344\t  },\n345\t  {\n346\t    \"name\": \"item_generation_calibration\",\n347\t    \"description\": \"Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions using LLM generation with construct constraints. Calibrate via student simulation. Compare difficulty prediction approaches.\\n\",\n348\t    \"results\": {\n349\t      \"n_items_generated\": 400,\n350\t      \"n_subjects_covered\": 28,\n351\t      \"n_items_passing_quality_filter\": 312,\n352\t      \"quality_pass_rate\": 0.78,\n353\t      \"construct_alignment_human_eval\": 0.81,\n354\t      \"construct_alignment_n_raters\": 3,\n355\t      \"construct_alignment_krippendorf_alpha\": 0.68,\n356\t      \"cognitive_level_match_rate\": 0.73,\n357\t      \"difficulty_rho_prompt_based\": 0.32,\n358\t      \"difficulty_rho_student_sim\": 0.58,\n359\t      \"difficulty_rho_student_sim_ci\": [\n360\t        0.49,\n361\t        0.66\n362\t      ],\n363\t      \"difficulty_rho_target\": 0.75,\n364\t      \"difficulty_rmse_prompt\": 0.51,\n365\t      \"difficulty_rmse_student_sim\": 0.34,\n366\t      \"holdout_panel_n_models\": 50,\n367\t      \"holdout_panel_correlation\": 0.61,\n368\t      \"discrimination_mean_generated\": 0.87,\n369\t      \"discrimination_mean_original\": 1.14,\n370\t      \"discrimination_gap\": -0.27,\n371\t      \"distractor_quality_mean\": 0.62,\n372\t      \"distractor_quality_original\": 0.71,\n373\t      \"generation_model\": \"GPT-4-turbo\",\n374\t      \"cost_per_item_usd\": 0.42\n375\t    },\n376\t    \"tables\": [\n377\t      {\n378\t        \"name\": \"Table S11: Item generation quality metrics\",\n379\t        \"description\": \"Overall and by-subject quality pass rate, construct alignment, difficulty correlation (prompt vs student-sim vs holdout), discrimination comparison.\\n\"\n380\t      }\n381\t    ],\n382\t    \"figures\": [],\n383\t    \"analysis\": \"Item generation calibration is the second pillar of the regeneration claim (original Claim 3), and its below-target performance confirms the regeneration pathway is premature. The difficulty calibration gap (ρ=0.58 vs target 0.75) is the binding constraint — even with perfect equating (plan_005), imprecise replacement items would degrade benchmark validity. The discrimination gap (−0.27) is equally concerning: lower-discrimination items provide less measurement information per item, meaning a regenerated benchmark would need MORE items to achieve equivalent precision. In median tier, this negative result supports the paper's pivot from \\\"regeneration\\\" to \\\"diagnostic lens\\\" — DIF's value is in characterizing what changed, not in enabling automated item replacement. One paragraph in appendix, framed as evidence that \\\"automated benchmark maintenance requires psychometric calibration advances beyond current LLM generation capability.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, item generation achieves mechanical success but psychometric inadequacy. Quality pass rate (78%) and construct alignment (0.81) indicate generated items are topically appropriate, but difficulty calibration is the bottleneck. Prompt-based targeting (ρ=0.32) is consistent with published estimates (0.10-0.45); student simulation improves to ρ=0.58 but falls short of the 0.75-0.82 benchmark from the referenced paper. The gap likely reflects MMLU's domain breadth — student simulation models are trained on specific domains and don't transfer well across MMLU's 57 subjects. Generated items have lower discrimination (0.87 vs 1.14 original), meaning they separate high- and low-ability models less effectively. Distractor quality (0.62 vs 0.71) is also lower — generated distractors attract fewer models, making items easier to guess. The holdout panel correlation (0.61) confirms difficulty mismatch isn't just calibration error but reflects genuine behavioral differences between generated and original items. Inter-rater reliability (Krippendorff's α=0.68) is acceptable but not strong, introducing human evaluation noise. In median tier, this is a below-benchmark result: the pipeline works but doesn't produce items with sufficient psychometric properties for valid equating. Combined with plan_005's equating quality degradation, the regeneration pathway is not viable with current technology. Brief appendix mention as negative pilot.\\n\"\n384\t  },\n385\t  {\n386\t    \"name\": \"cumulative_bayesian_dif\",\n387\t    \"description\": \"Simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006) across 4 successive benchmark administrations. Characterize power accumulation for medium DIF effects.\\n\",\n388\t    \"results\": {\n389\t      \"n_administrations\": 4,\n390\t      \"n_items_per_admin\": 3000,\n391\t      \"n_models_per_admin\": 500,\n392\t      \"effect_size_delta\": 1.0,\n393\t      \"single_admin_power\": 0.41,\n394\t      \"cumulative_2admin_power\": 0.53,\n395\t      \"cumulative_3admin_power\": 0.62,\n396\t      \"cumulative_4admin_power\": 0.68,\n397\t      \"target_3admin_power\": 0.7,\n398\t      \"target_met\": false,\n399\t      \"power_gain_per_admin\": [\n400\t        0.12,\n401\t        0.09,\n402\t        0.06\n403\t      ],\n404\t      \"prior_flat_power_4admin\": 0.65,\n405\t      \"prior_informative_power_4admin\": 0.68,\n406\t      \"prior_sensitivity_delta\": 0.03,\n407\t      \"fpr_single\": 0.048,\n408\t      \"fpr_cumulative_4\": 0.052,\n409\t      \"fpr_inflation\": 0.004,\n410\t      \"bayes_factor_threshold\": 3.0,\n411\t      \"pct_items_bf_above_3_single\": 0.28,\n412\t      \"pct_items_bf_above_3_cumulative4\": 0.51,\n413\t      \"computation_time_minutes\": 14,\n414\t      \"convergence_diagnostics_rhat_max\": 1.03\n415\t    },\n416\t    \"tables\": [\n417\t      {\n418\t        \"name\": \"Table S12: Cumulative Bayesian DIF power across administrations\",\n419\t        \"description\": \"Rows: 1-4 administrations. Columns: power (flat prior), power (informative prior), FPR, Bayes factor >3 proportion, cumulative data points.\\n\"\n420\t      }\n421\t    ],\n422\t    \"figures\": [],\n423\t    \"analysis\": \"Cumulative Bayesian DIF is theoretically appealing for longitudinal benchmark monitoring but empirically modest in median tier. The power gains are real but slow — 4 administrations are needed to approach what a single larger-cohort MH analysis achieves at δ≥1.4. The practical implication is that temporal accumulation supplements rather than replaces cross-sectional DIF. In median tier, this goes to appendix with framing: \\\"Bayesian evidence accumulation is a promising direction for detecting gradual item degradation, but current power gains are incremental. Practical deployment requires automated pipeline integration with leaderboard update cycles.\\\" This connects to the \\\"actionable recommendation\\\" the narrative guidance demands.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, cumulative Bayesian DIF shows the expected monotonic power improvement but falls short of targets. Power rises from 0.41 (single administration) to 0.68 (4 administrations) for medium effects (δ=1.0) — below the 0.70 target at 3 administrations (0.62). The shortfall reflects realistic MMLU item parameters (a_mean=1.2, lower discrimination than textbook examples) and the relatively small cohort sizes per administration (N=500). Diminishing returns are evident: +0.12 from admin 1→2, +0.09 from 2→3, +0.06 from 3→4 — each additional administration provides less lift as the posterior narrows. Prior sensitivity is minimal (flat vs informative: Δ=0.03), suggesting the data dominates the prior even after 4 administrations — a positive robustness finding but also indicating the priors aren't adding much value. FPR inflation is negligible (+0.004 over 4 administrations), confirming the Bayesian framework controls Type I error well. The simulation is internally clean but has two fundamental limitations: (1) it's simulation-only — no real multi-administration LLM benchmark data exists to validate against; (2) it assumes stationary DIF patterns across administrations, which is unlikely in the rapidly evolving LLM landscape. In median tier, this is a correct but incremental simulation study that belongs in the appendix as motivation for longitudinal DIF monitoring, not a main contribution.\\n\"\n424\t  },\n425\t  {\n426\t    \"name\": \"graceful_degradation_analysis\",\n427\t    \"description\": \"Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement. Validity metrics (ranking stability, score reliability, construct coverage) as function of replacement rate.\\n\",\n428\t    \"results\": {\n429\t      \"replacement_rates_tested\": [\n430\t        0.05,\n431\t        0.1,\n432\t        0.15,\n433\t        0.2,\n434\t        0.25,\n435\t        0.3,\n436\t        0.5\n437\t      ],\n438\t      \"rho_at_5pct\": 0.994,\n439\t      \"rho_at_10pct\": 0.987,\n440\t      \"rho_at_15pct\": 0.976,\n441\t      \"rho_at_20pct\": 0.971,\n442\t      \"rho_at_25pct\": 0.958,\n443\t      \"rho_at_30pct\": 0.948,\n444\t      \"rho_at_50pct\": 0.923,\n445\t      \"reliability_at_5pct\": 0.989,\n446\t      \"reliability_at_10pct\": 0.981,\n447\t      \"reliability_at_15pct\": 0.972,\n448\t      \"reliability_at_20pct\": 0.964,\n449\t      \"reliability_at_25pct\": 0.951,\n450\t      \"reliability_at_30pct\": 0.94,\n451\t      \"reliability_at_50pct\": 0.912,\n452\t      \"construct_coverage_at_5pct\": 0.97,\n453\t      \"construct_coverage_at_10pct\": 0.94,\n454\t      \"construct_coverage_at_20pct\": 0.89,\n455\t      \"construct_coverage_at_30pct\": 0.83,\n456\t      \"construct_coverage_at_50pct\": 0.72,\n457\t      \"critical_threshold_rho_095\": 0.27,\n458\t      \"critical_threshold_reliability_095\": 0.24,\n459\t      \"critical_threshold_coverage_090\": 0.18,\n460\t      \"degradation_linear_fit_rho_r2\": 0.96,\n461\t      \"degradation_linear_fit_reliability_r2\": 0.98,\n462\t      \"random_vs_dif_targeted_replacement\": {\n463\t        \"random_30pct_rho\": 0.948,\n464\t        \"dif_targeted_30pct_rho\": 0.952,\n465\t        \"difference\": 0.004\n466\t      },\n467\t      \"n_bootstrap_iterations\": 1000\n468\t    },\n469\t    \"tables\": [\n470\t      {\n471\t        \"name\": \"Table S13: Validity metrics under progressive replacement\",\n472\t        \"description\": \"Replacement rate × ranking stability (ρ), score reliability (α), construct coverage. Critical thresholds highlighted. Random vs DIF-targeted comparison at 30%.\\n\"\n473\t      }\n474\t    ],\n475\t    \"figures\": [],\n476\t    \"analysis\": \"Graceful degradation quantifies the practical limits of item replacement for MMLU benchmark maintenance. The 27% critical threshold (ρ<0.95) provides a concrete operational bound that benchmark developers can use. The finding that DIF-targeted and random replacement produce similar degradation reinforces plan_017/020's conclusion that DIF items are not the most \\\"important\\\" items — they're items whose functioning changed, not items that carry the most ranking information. In median tier, this is clean supplementary material that supports the \\\"instability ≠ invalidity\\\" framing with quantitative bounds. Appendix.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, graceful degradation shows clean, near-linear validity loss under progressive replacement. The key finding: ρ drops below 0.95 at ~27% replacement rate, just below the 31% DIF-C item proportion. This means replacing ALL DIF items would marginally violate the 0.95 stability threshold (ρ=0.948 at 30%). However, this uses random replacement items (not calibrated), so it measures a worst-case scenario. Construct coverage degrades faster than ranking stability (drops below 0.90 at 18%) because randomly replacing items within a subject can eliminate coverage of specific cognitive skills within that domain. The DIF-targeted vs random replacement comparison (0.952 vs 0.948 at 30%) shows negligible difference — DIF items are not preferentially \\\"important\\\" for ranking, which is consistent with plan_017's finding that DIF removal is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy. In median tier, this provides useful quantitative context for the \\\"instability ≠ invalidity\\\" framing: it shows that MMLU can tolerate substantial item replacement without catastrophic validity loss. The 27% critical threshold is close enough to 31% that it adds narrative tension — DIF items are at the boundary of safe replacement. Clean supplementary analysis.\\n\"\n477\t  },\n478\t  {\n479\t    \"name\": \"item_focused_trees\",\n480\t    \"description\": \"Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) using model release date as continuous covariate. Compare DIF detection with continuous vs categorical (MH) approach. Nonlinear DIF trend detection.\\n\",\n481\t    \"results\": {\n482\t      \"n_items_analyzed\": 12508,\n483\t      \"ift_significant_items\": 4102,\n484\t      \"mh_significant_items_ets_c\": 3918,\n485\t      \"overlap_items\": 3064,\n486\t      \"ift_unique_items\": 1038,\n487\t      \"mh_unique_items\": 854,\n488\t      \"overlap_jaccard\": 0.74,\n489\t      \"release_date_split_point_modal\": \"2023-09\",\n490\t      \"release_date_split_point_range\": [\n491\t        \"2023-06\",\n492\t        \"2024-01\"\n493\t      ],\n494\t      \"nonlinear_trend_items_pct\": 8.3,\n495\t      \"nonlinear_trend_items_n\": 1038,\n496\t      \"monotonic_increasing_dif_pct\": 52.1,\n497\t      \"monotonic_decreasing_dif_pct\": 39.6,\n498\t      \"u_shaped_dif_pct\": 5.1,\n499\t      \"inverted_u_dif_pct\": 3.2,\n500\t      \"tree_depth_median\": 2,\n501\t      \"tree_depth_max\": 4,\n502\t      \"model_convergence_rate\": 0.94,\n503\t      \"computation_hours\": 3.2,\n504\t      \"ift_effect_size_correlation_with_mh\": 0.81,\n505\t      \"ift_unique_items_mean_delta_mh\": 1.12\n506\t    },\n507\t    \"tables\": [\n508\t      {\n509\t        \"name\": \"Table S14: IFT vs MH DIF detection comparison\",\n510\t        \"description\": \"Cross-tabulation of IFT-flagged vs MH-flagged items. Overlap, unique-to-each, Jaccard. Mean DIF effect size in each cell. IFT-unique items characterized by gradual trend.\\n\"\n511\t      },\n512\t      {\n513\t        \"name\": \"Table S15: Non-linear DIF trend typology\",\n514\t        \"description\": \"DIF trend types (monotonic increasing/decreasing, U-shaped, inverted-U) with prevalence, example subjects, and interpretation. 8.3% non-linear.\\n\"\n515\t      }\n516\t    ],\n517\t    \"figures\": [],\n518\t    \"analysis\": \"IFT confirms MH's core finding from a different methodological angle: MMLU items exhibit substantial temporal DIF regardless of whether cohorts are defined categorically or continuously. The Jaccard of 0.74 is high enough to validate MH but low enough to show MH misses some gradual-onset DIF items. The nonlinear trends (8.3%) are genuinely interesting — they suggest some items' functioning is non-monotonic over model generations, possibly reflecting specific model family effects that come and go. However, this is supplementary nuance, not a main finding. In median tier, IFT goes to appendix as robustness check supporting the statement: \\\"Temporal DIF detection is robust to cohort definition methodology; continuous approaches capture additional gradual-onset items but do not qualitatively change the instability finding.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, Item-Focused Trees detect 4,102 items with significant DIF using continuous release date, compared to 3,918 from categorical MH — a Jaccard of 0.74 shows substantial overlap. The 1,038 IFT-unique items have mean Δ_MH=1.12, below the ETS C threshold (|Δ|≥1.5) but identified by IFT because their DIF trend is gradual and monotonic — individually they're ETS B (moderate) but collectively they represent consistent temporal drift. The 854 MH-unique items tend to have abrupt DIF patterns at the temporal boundary that IFT's recursive partitioning smooths over. The modal split point (2023-09) is earlier than MH's categorical boundary (2024-01), suggesting the onset of DIF for many items predates the arbitrary cohort split. 8.3% of items show nonlinear DIF trends (U-shaped or inverted-U), which neither MH nor linear IFT captures well — these are items that functioned differently in mid-2023 models but reverted by 2024, possibly reflecting transient training data or architecture trends. The effect size correlation (0.81 with MH) confirms both methods capture the same underlying signal with different sensitivity profiles. In median tier, IFT provides useful robustness evidence that the core DIF finding is not an artifact of arbitrary cohort boundaries. The nonlinear trends add nuance but are too rare (8.3%) to qualitatively change the story. Appendix robustness check.\\n\"\n519\t  },\n520\t  {\n521\t    \"name\": \"architecture_stratified_dif\",\n522\t    \"description\": \"Run DIF within architecture-homogeneous subpopulation (decoder-only only) to test whether IRT fit improves and whether architecture heterogeneity contributes to spurious DIF.\\n\",\n523\t    \"results\": {\n524\t      \"total_models\": 5227,\n525\t      \"decoder_only_models\": 5226,\n526\t      \"non_decoder_models\": 1,\n527\t      \"encoder_decoder_models\": 0,\n528\t      \"decoder_only_pct\": 99.98,\n529\t      \"decoder_only_temporal_c_pct\": 31.3,\n530\t      \"full_population_temporal_c_pct\": 31.3,\n531\t      \"jaccard_decoder_vs_full\": 1.0,\n532\t      \"delta_mh_correlation_decoder_vs_full\": 0.9999,\n533\t      \"rmsea_decoder_only\": 0.041,\n534\t      \"rmsea_full\": 0.041,\n535\t      \"cfi_decoder_only\": 0.82,\n536\t      \"cfi_full\": 0.82,\n537\t      \"tli_decoder_only\": 0.8,\n538\t      \"tli_full\": 0.8,\n539\t      \"srmr_decoder_only\": 0.048,\n540\t      \"srmr_full\": 0.048,\n541\t      \"irt_lz_pct_above_2_decoder\": 98.1,\n542\t      \"irt_lz_pct_above_2_full\": 98.2,\n543\t      \"architecture_confound_effect\": \"none (single non-decoder model)\",\n544\t      \"single_non_decoder_model\": \"flan-t5-xl or similar\",\n545\t      \"c_pct_change_removing_non_decoder\": 0.0\n546\t    },\n547\t    \"tables\": [\n548\t      {\n549\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: Architecture composition and DIF robustness\",\n550\t        \"description\": \"Architecture breakdown (decoder-only: 5226, other: 1). DIF statistics: C%, Jaccard, IRT fit indices for full vs decoder-only populations. All identical.\\n\"\n551\t      }\n552\t    ],\n553\t    \"figures\": [],\n554\t    \"analysis\": \"Architecture stratification dissolves a potential reviewer concern with minimal effort. The 99.98% decoder-only composition means architecture heterogeneity is empirically irrelevant for METABENCH MMLU. This should be presented proactively in §4.1 (immediately after the main DIF result) to prevent the reviewer from even forming the objection. The persistent IRT misfit (CFI=0.82) after removing the single non-decoder model is used as evidence that misfit is structural (multidimensionality) rather than compositional (architecture), supporting the §5 limitations expansion. In median tier, this is a clean 1-paragraph robustness check in the main body: \\\"Architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue: METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5,226/5,227); excluding the single non-decoder model produces identical DIF results (Jaccard=1.0). IRT misfit persists (CFI=0.82), confirming it reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality, not architecture mixing (see §5).\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, architecture stratification is a complete non-issue — and this is itself a useful finding. METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models), with a single encoder-decoder model (likely a Flan-T5 variant or similar). Removing this one model changes literally nothing: Jaccard=1.0, C% identical to 4 decimal places, Δ_MH correlation=0.9999. IRT fit diagnostics (RMSEA=0.041, CFI=0.82, |Lz|>2 for 98.1% of models) are identical in the decoder-only subset because the fit problem is structural (57-subject multidimensionality), not compositional (architecture mixing). This result serves two purposes: (1) it completely preempts the reviewer concern \\\"maybe DIF is detecting architecture differences, not temporal changes\\\" — the population IS architecture-homogeneous; (2) it honestly demonstrates that IRT misfit persists even in a homogeneous population, confirming the misfit is inherent to applying unidimensional IRT to multidimensional MMLU. The first purpose is a defense; the second is an honest limitation that feeds into the §5 expansion. Main body robustness check (1-2 paragraphs in §4.1).\\n\"\n555\t  },\n556\t  {\n557\t    \"name\": \"semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\",\n558\t    \"description\": \"Inject known contamination into METABENCH response matrix and measure DIF detection power under controlled ground truth. Cross-subject vs within-subject matching comparison. Dose-response characterization.\\n\",\n559\t    \"results\": {\n560\t      \"n_clean_items_selected\": 500,\n561\t      \"n_injected_items\": 150,\n562\t      \"injection_rates_tested\": [\n563\t        0.0,\n564\t        0.3,\n565\t        0.5,\n566\t        0.7,\n567\t        1.0\n568\t      ],\n569\t      \"temporal_cohort_standard_matching\": {\n570\t        \"baseline_fpr\": 0.298,\n571\t        \"tpr_p00\": 0.298,\n572\t        \"tpr_p03\": 0.483,\n573\t        \"tpr_p05\": 0.596,\n574\t        \"tpr_p07\": 0.812,\n575\t        \"tpr_p10\": 0.972,\n576\t        \"delta_tpr_p03\": 0.185,\n577\t        \"delta_tpr_p05\": 0.298,\n578\t        \"delta_tpr_p07\": 0.514,\n579\t        \"delta_tpr_p10\": 0.674,\n580\t        \"fpr_inflation_p05\": 0.147,\n581\t        \"fpr_inflation_p10\": 0.318\n582\t      },\n583\t      \"temporal_cohort_external_matching\": {\n584\t        \"baseline_fpr\": 0.314,\n585\t        \"tpr_p00\": 0.326,\n586\t        \"tpr_p03\": 0.548,\n587\t        \"tpr_p05\": 0.834,\n588\t        \"tpr_p07\": 0.99,\n589\t        \"tpr_p10\": 1.0,\n590\t        \"delta_tpr_p03\": 0.222,\n591\t        \"delta_tpr_p05\": 0.508,\n592\t        \"delta_tpr_p07\": 0.664,\n593\t        \"delta_tpr_p10\": 0.674,\n594\t        \"delta_fpr\": 0.0,\n595\t        \"fpr_stable\": true\n596\t      },\n597\t      \"within_subject_matching_fpr_inflation\": 0.186,\n598\t      \"cross_subject_matching_delta_fpr\": 0.0,\n599\t      \"dose_response_r2\": 0.97,\n600\t      \"dose_response_slope\": 0.68,\n601\t      \"ability_cohort_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.302,\n602\t      \"matching_contamination_confirmed\": true,\n603\t      \"inf_delta_bug_fixed\": true\n604\t    },\n605\t    \"tables\": [\n606\t      {\n607\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: Semi-synthetic injection detection power\",\n608\t        \"description\": \"Injection rate (p=0.0 to 1.0) × matching strategy (standard, external/cross-subject, within-subject). TPR, ΔTPR, FPR, ΔFPR. Highlights cross-subject ΔFPR=0.000.\\n\"\n609\t      },\n610\t      {\n611\t        \"name\": \"Table S16: Matching variable contamination analysis\",\n612\t        \"description\": \"Detailed FPR breakdown by matching strategy and injection rate. Shows within-subject FPR inflation mechanism and cross-subject immunity.\\n\"\n613\t      }\n614\t    ],\n615\t    \"figures\": [],\n616\t    \"analysis\": \"Semi-synthetic injection is the strongest methodological contribution in median tier. It solves the ground truth circularity problem (Li et al. proxy labels can't validate item-level differential detectors) by injecting known differential contamination into real response matrices. The cross-subject matching insight is a practical innovation with immediate applicability: any researcher applying MH-DIF to multi-domain benchmarks should use cross-domain scores as matching variables. The ΔFPR=0.000 result is unusually clean — it's not \\\"small FPR inflation\\\" but literally zero across all injection rates and all 5 tested subjects. This robustness makes it a credible methodological recommendation. The dose-response curve provides the operating characteristics a practitioner needs: at p=0.5 exploitation rate, cross-subject DIF achieves ΔTPR≈0.50 — meaningful detection power that complements the raw ΔTPR with calibrated ground truth. In median tier, this is the contribution most likely to survive reviewer scrutiny because it's self-contained, internally validated, and immediately useful. §4 main body, Claim 3.\\n\\nIn median tier, semi-synthetic injection is the cleanest methodological contribution. ΔTPR at p=0.5 is +0.508 (external matching), demonstrating DIF has genuine detection power for differential contamination when ground truth is controlled. The dose-response (R²=0.97) follows a near-linear trajectory from p=0.3 to p=1.0, confirming the relationship between contamination intensity and detection power is well-behaved. The critical methodological insight is cross-subject matching: when the MH matching variable (total score) is computed on OTHER subjects, FPR is completely stable (ΔFPR=0.000) across all injection rates. Within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable because injected correct answers inflate the total score on the same items being tested, creating a circular dependency (FPR inflation +0.186). This cross-subject insight is non-obvious — it requires understanding that MH conditions on total score, and that contamination inflates total score on affected items, creating artificial ability group shifts. The insight generalizes to any multi-domain benchmark where cross-domain scores can serve as matching variables. Standard matching (total score on ALL items including tested ones) shows intermediate behavior — some FPR inflation (+0.318 at p=1.0) but less than within-subject because contamination signal is diluted across 57 subjects. In median tier, this is a main body result (§4, Claim 3) with the cross-subject matching as the primary methodological recommendation.\\n\"\n617\t  }\n618\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7954f472-5bda-40b0-9628-70dc608e1f91","uuid":"574a58ca-35f4-4aea-8b5f-2c359ff04465","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:59:45.196Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T042647/median/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"real_data_dif_mmlu\",\n    \"description\": \"Apply MH-DIF analysis to METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 items). Temporal cohort (2023 vs 2024-H1), ability-quintile cohort, within-family confound control, domain-specificity analysis, Li et al. proxy validation, SIBTEST non-uniform DIF check.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"temporal_ets_c_pct\": 31.3,\n      \"temporal_ets_b_pct\": 20.1,\n      \"temporal_ets_a_pct\": 48.6,\n      \"temporal_n_ref\": 1253,\n      \"temporal_n_foc\": 656,\n      \"temporal_fpr_li_clean\": 0.318,\n      \"temporal_enrichment\": 0.96,\n      \"ability_ets_c_pct\": 2.9,\n      \"ability_enrichment\": 1.19,\n      \"temporal_ability_jaccard\": 0.014,\n      \"within_family_llama2v3_c_pct\": 61.3,\n      \"within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct\": 38.7,\n      \"domain_spearman_rho\": 0.307,\n      \"domain_spearman_p\": 0.02,\n      \"sanity_random_split_c_pct\": 0.18,\n      \"sibtest_nonuniform_additional_pct\": 11.2,\n      \"sibtest_nonuniform_items\": 1406,\n      \"mh_sibtest_uniform_jaccard\": 0.71,\n      \"sibtest_total_flagged_pct\": 42.5,\n      \"sibtest_crossing_icc_pct\": 7.8,\n      \"sibtest_amplifying_pct\": 3.4,\n      \"negative_discrimination_items\": 1927,\n      \"negative_discrimination_pct\": 15.4,\n      \"sensitivity_with_neg_disc_removed_c_pct\": 29.8\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 1: DIF prevalence across cohort definitions and item filtering\",\n        \"description\": \"4-way sensitivity matrix: {temporal, ability} × {with, without negative-discrimination items}. Shows temporal C%=31.3% (29.8% without neg-disc items), ability C%=2.9%. Includes ETS A/B/C breakdown and SIBTEST non-uniform counts for each cell.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: Within-family DIF analysis\",\n        \"description\": \"Family pairs (Llama-2 vs Llama-3, Mistral vs Mixtral) with C%, item overlap with temporal DIF, and interpretation (capability gain vs contamination differential).\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S1: SIBTEST concordance with MH\",\n        \"description\": \"Cross-tabulation of MH-flagged vs SIBTEST-flagged items. Uniform-only (MH∩SIBTEST): 2,789. MH-only: 1,129. SIBTEST non-uniform only: 1,406. Neither: 7,184. Jaccard=0.71.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The core finding is robust: ~31% of MMLU items function differently across model generations, and this is not a statistical artifact (sanity check: 0.18%), not driven by ability differences (ability cohort: 2.9%), and predominantly item-specific (domain improvement explains ≤7.6%). The SIBTEST extension reveals MH underestimates total DIF by ~11pp, requiring a new paragraph in §3 acknowledging non-uniform DIF as an MH limitation. The framing becomes: \\\"MH captures the dominant uniform DIF signal (31.3% of items); SIBTEST concordance (Jaccard=0.71) confirms the additional ~11% non-uniform DIF items exist but do not qualitatively change the instability finding — if anything, they strengthen it.\\\" This is honest, preemptive, and turns a potential weakness into evidence of thoroughness.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier the core instability finding (31.3% ETS C) holds exactly — this is the anchor result around which the paper is built. Random-split sanity check (0.18%) and ability-cohort orthogonality (Jaccard=0.014) confirm the signal is temporal, not statistical artifact. However, SIBTEST reveals 11.2% additional items with significant non-uniform DIF (crossing ICCs where the item favors one cohort at low ability but the other at high ability). MH, by design, only detects uniform DIF (constant direction across ability levels). The MH-SIBTEST Jaccard of 0.71 shows MH captures the dominant signal but misses ~1,400 items with crossing patterns. This is expected for a 60-year-old method applied to a heterogeneous LLM population, but requires honest reporting: total temporal instability is ~42.5%, not 31.3%. The uniform:non-uniform ratio (3:1) supports framing MH as capturing the dominant mode. Crossing ICCs (7.8%) are particularly interesting — these items favor older models at low ability but newer models at high ability, potentially reflecting genuine capability structure changes. Domain correlation (ρ=0.307) remains modest; the item-level decomposition from plan_019 (≤2.4pp/7.6% explained) is the stronger defense. Within-family analysis shows Llama-2→3 transition has extreme DIF (61.3% C), consistent with a major architecture+data shift, while Mistral→Mixtral is lower (38.7%), reflecting the mixture-of-experts change being less disruptive to item functioning.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"rl_robustness_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Validate DIF robustness to RL training. SFT baseline, alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO, N=477), and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO, N=13) cohort comparisons. Traditional detector degradation analysis. Power analysis for partial exploitation rates.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"sft_cohort_n\": 312,\n      \"alignment_rl_cohort_n\": 477,\n      \"reasoning_rl_models_n\": 13,\n      \"reasoning_rl_models_list\": \"DeepSeek-R1-7B, DeepSeek-R1-32B, Qwen2.5-7B-RL, Qwen2.5-14B-RL, Qwen2.5-32B-RL, Llama-3.1-8B-GRPO, Llama-3.1-70B-GRPO, Phi-3-mini-GRPO, Mistral-7B-PPO, Yi-1.5-9B-RL, Gemma-2-9B-RL, InternLM2-7B-RL, MAP-Neo-7B-RL\",\n      \"dif_flag_retention_sft_vs_alignrl_jaccard\": 0.83,\n      \"dif_flag_retention_sft_vs_reasonrl_jaccard\": 0.69,\n      \"dif_flag_retention_alignrl_vs_reasonrl_jaccard\": 0.72,\n      \"constat_auroc_sft\": 0.72,\n      \"constat_auroc_alignrl\": 0.68,\n      \"constat_auroc_reasonrl\": 0.51,\n      \"constat_auroc_reasonrl_ci\": [\n        0.38,\n        0.64\n      ],\n      \"mink_auroc_sft\": 0.69,\n      \"mink_auroc_alignrl\": 0.64,\n      \"mink_auroc_reasonrl\": 0.48,\n      \"mink_auroc_reasonrl_ci\": [\n        0.34,\n        0.62\n      ],\n      \"dif_cohort_c_pct_with_rl_added\": 30.8,\n      \"dif_cohort_c_pct_without_rl\": 31.3,\n      \"delong_p_constat_sft_vs_reasonrl\": 0.04,\n      \"delong_p_mink_sft_vs_reasonrl\": 0.03,\n      \"tost_equivalence_dif_retention_p\": 0.08,\n      \"power_analysis_exploitation_p04_tpr\": 0.31,\n      \"power_analysis_exploitation_p06_tpr\": 0.47,\n      \"power_analysis_exploitation_p07_tpr\": 0.58,\n      \"power_analysis_exploitation_p10_tpr\": 0.72,\n      \"detection_boundary_80pct_power\": \"p≈0.85, δ≈1.7\",\n      \"bootstrap_ci_retention_jaccard_reasonrl\": [\n        0.54,\n        0.81\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S2: DIF retention across training paradigms\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows: SFT, Alignment-RL, Reasoning-RL. Columns: N, cohort C%, Jaccard vs SFT baseline, bootstrap 95% CI. Shows stability for alignment-RL, uncertainty for reasoning-RL.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S3: Traditional detector degradation on RL models\",\n        \"description\": \"ConStat and Min-K% AUROC by training paradigm (SFT/Align-RL/Reason-RL), with DeLong p-values and 95% CIs. Shows significant degradation on reasoning-RL.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The two-level validation design (group-level DIF vs model-level traditional) is theoretically clean but empirically asymmetric: the alignment-RL arm has adequate power (N=477) while the reasoning-RL arm (N=13) is severely underpowered. In median tier, this asymmetry is exactly what a careful reviewer would flag — the claim that DIF is RL-robust rests on the less interesting arm (DPO doesn't use PPO clipping anyway) while the critical arm (GRPO/PPO) is inconclusive. The power analysis honestly characterizes the detection boundary but doesn't resolve the core question. Paper strategy: present alignment-RL result in supplementary as \\\"confirmed robustness to offline preference optimization,\\\" acknowledge reasoning-RL as the key open question in §5 limitations, and frame the power analysis as roadmap for future validation. Do NOT claim RL robustness as a contribution.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, the RL robustness story is partially supported but critically underpowered for the reasoning-RL arm. Alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO) cohort shows good DIF flag retention (Jaccard=0.83 vs SFT) — expected because DPO doesn't use PPO clipping and doesn't produce the NLL gap contraction that conceals contamination. Adding 477 alignment-RL models to the focal cohort barely changes C% (31.3→30.8%), confirming DIF is robust to this RL variant. The reasoning-RL comparison is the problem: N=13 models yields Jaccard=0.69 with a bootstrap CI of [0.54, 0.81] — too wide to distinguish \\\"partial degradation\\\" from \\\"no degradation\\\" or \\\"serious degradation.\\\" TOST equivalence test fails (p=0.08), meaning we cannot formally claim DIF retention is equivalent across RL types. Traditional detectors DO degrade on reasoning-RL (ConStat AUROC 0.72→0.51, Min-K% 0.69→0.48), and DeLong tests confirm these drops are significant (p<0.05) — but this comparison is apples-to-oranges (group-level DIF vs model-level detectors). Power analysis shows DIF's detection boundary at p≈0.85 exploitation rate for 80% power — reasonable for full memorization but weak for partial exploitation. The N=13 reasoning-RL limitation is fundamental: more open-weight GRPO/PPO models simply don't exist yet in METABENCH. This experiment provides theoretical motivation but not empirical proof. Demoted to limitations section with honest framing: \\\"Alignment-RL robustness confirmed (N=477); reasoning-RL robustness remains an open question due to small sample (N=13). As reasoning-RL models proliferate, this validation becomes feasible.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"constat_perturbation_eval\",\n    \"description\": \"Independent ConStat-style perturbation testing on MMLU. Generate semantically equivalent reformulations for 218 items, compare original vs reformulated performance, apply FDR-controlled detection, characterize power by training paradigm.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_paraphrased\": 218,\n      \"paraphrase_method\": \"GPT-4 with construct-preservation constraints\",\n      \"paraphrase_semantic_similarity_mean\": 0.891,\n      \"paraphrase_semantic_similarity_std\": 0.062,\n      \"paraphrase_quality_human_eval_accuracy\": 0.76,\n      \"paraphrase_quality_human_eval_n_raters\": 3,\n      \"constat_detection_rate_overall\": 0.28,\n      \"constat_detection_rate_non_rl\": 0.37,\n      \"constat_detection_rate_alignment_rl\": 0.31,\n      \"constat_detection_rate_reasoning_rl\": 0.11,\n      \"constat_fdr_controlled\": 0.08,\n      \"constat_fpr\": 0.06,\n      \"constat_fpr_ci\": [\n        0.03,\n        0.1\n      ],\n      \"effect_size_mean_overall\": 0.64,\n      \"effect_size_mean_non_rl\": 0.82,\n      \"effect_size_mean_alignment_rl\": 0.71,\n      \"effect_size_mean_reasoning_rl\": 0.29,\n      \"dif_constat_overlap_jaccard\": 0.23,\n      \"dif_constat_union_coverage\": 0.41,\n      \"items_constat_only\": 87,\n      \"items_dif_only\": 312,\n      \"items_both\": 64\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S4: ConStat detection power by training paradigm\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows: Non-RL, Alignment-RL, Reasoning-RL, Overall. Columns: N, detection rate, mean effect size, FDR. Shows clear degradation gradient from non-RL to reasoning-RL.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"ConStat replication confirms the literature's findings on non-RL models but adds limited value beyond what's already published. The low DIF-ConStat overlap (Jaccard=0.23) is the most interesting finding — it demonstrates that DIF and perturbation approaches target fundamentally different contamination modes. This supports the paper's positioning of DIF as a novel lens rather than a competing method. However, 218 items is a small subset of MMLU's 12,508, and paraphrase quality concerns limit confidence. In median tier, this is appendix material that serves primarily to contextualize DIF's contribution relative to existing methods.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, ConStat provides a useful but limited baseline comparison. The 37% detection rate on non-RL models shows perturbation sensitivity is a real signal, but the 11% rate on reasoning-RL models confirms the theoretical prediction that models trained with online RL develop robustness to surface-level reformulations. The DIF-ConStat Jaccard of 0.23 is low, indicating the two methods detect largely different item sets — DIF catches items with differential difficulty across cohorts (conditioning on ability), while ConStat catches items where models exploit surface features (verbatim memorization). This low overlap is actually informative: it shows the two approaches target different contamination mechanisms. However, paraphrase quality (human eval 0.76) means ~24% of reformulations may introduce construct-irrelevant variance — a methodological weakness a reviewer could exploit. In median tier, ConStat results go to appendix as baseline comparison, supporting the narrative that \\\"perturbation methods and DIF capture orthogonal contamination dimensions, but perturbation methods degrade under RL.\\\" The low Jaccard is cited in §2 Related Work to position DIF as complementary, not competing.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"dvd_generation_variance_eval\",\n    \"description\": \"DVD generation variance analysis on MMLU. Temperature-sampled responses (50 per item) from 12 open-weight models. Bimodal distribution detection and variance-based contamination scoring.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_models_tested\": 12,\n      \"models_list\": \"Llama-2-7B, Llama-2-13B, Llama-3-8B, Mistral-7B-v0.1, Mistral-7B-v0.3, Phi-3-mini, Qwen-1.5-7B, Qwen-2-7B, Yi-1.5-9B, Gemma-7B, Gemma-2-9B, InternLM2-7B\",\n      \"n_items_sampled\": 200,\n      \"n_generations_per_item\": 50,\n      \"temperature\": 0.8,\n      \"bimodal_detection_rate\": 0.14,\n      \"bimodal_detection_ci\": [\n        0.09,\n        0.2\n      ],\n      \"variance_score_auroc\": 0.61,\n      \"variance_score_auroc_ci\": [\n        0.52,\n        0.7\n      ],\n      \"verbatim_contamination_sensitivity\": 0.43,\n      \"variant_contamination_sensitivity\": 0.18,\n      \"false_positive_rate\": 0.12,\n      \"dif_dvd_overlap_jaccard\": 0.15,\n      \"compute_cost_gpu_hours\": 62,\n      \"inference_failures_pct\": 3.2,\n      \"mcq_format_compliance_rate\": 0.91\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S5: DVD performance by model and contamination type\",\n        \"description\": \"12 models × {verbatim, variant, clean} items. Variance scores, bimodal detection rates, and AUROC per model. Shows model-level heterogeneity (some models more amenable to DVD than others, no clear pattern).\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"DVD's failure on MCQ benchmarks is an informative negative result. The method was designed for open-ended generation where contaminated models produce stereotyped outputs with low variance. MCQ format constrains all models to categorical outputs (A/B/C/D), compressing the variance signal that DVD relies on. This is a structural limitation, not a tuning issue. In median tier, this is cleanly negative and excluded from further analysis. One paragraph in appendix.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, DVD underperforms expectations. The bimodal distribution signal (14% of items) is real but rare — most contaminated items don't produce the clean bimodal (memorized vs non-memorized generation) pattern that DVD theory predicts, likely because MCQ format constrains generation variance (models commit to A/B/C/D, producing categorical rather than continuous distributions). AUROC of 0.61 with CI [0.52, 0.70] is barely above chance at the lower bound. Variant contamination sensitivity (0.18) is particularly disappointing — DVD's theoretical advantage over perturbation methods is detecting non-verbatim memorization, but this fails to materialize. The 62 GPU-hour cost for 12 models × 200 items × 50 generations is disproportionate to the signal strength. Format compliance issues (9% of generations don't produce valid MCQ answers) add noise. The DIF-DVD Jaccard of 0.15 shows almost no overlap, suggesting DVD and DIF detect unrelated item properties. In median tier, DVD is a clear negative result: the method doesn't port well from open-ended generation (its original domain) to MCQ benchmarks. Excluded from ensemble (plan_003c), brief appendix mention as negative finding.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"ensemble_fusion\",\n    \"description\": \"Fuse DIF + ConStat into 2-signal ensemble (DVD excluded due to near-chance performance). Calibrate thresholds, evaluate majority voting and weighted fusion, cross-validate.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"ensemble_signals\": 2,\n      \"ensemble_auroc_majority\": 0.73,\n      \"ensemble_auroc_weighted\": 0.77,\n      \"ensemble_auroc_weighted_ci\": [\n        0.7,\n        0.84\n      ],\n      \"dif_standalone_auroc\": 0.74,\n      \"constat_standalone_auroc\": 0.66,\n      \"improvement_over_best_single\": 0.03,\n      \"improvement_ci\": [\n        -0.02,\n        0.08\n      ],\n      \"ensemble_fpr\": 0.09,\n      \"ensemble_tpr_delta_ge_06\": 0.52,\n      \"ensemble_tpr_delta_ge_14\": 0.74,\n      \"fusion_weight_dif\": 0.62,\n      \"fusion_weight_constat\": 0.38,\n      \"cross_validation_5fold_mean_auroc\": 0.75,\n      \"cross_validation_5fold_std\": 0.04,\n      \"uniform_weight_auroc\": 0.74\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S6: Ensemble vs standalone detection performance\",\n        \"description\": \"Comparison of DIF-only, ConStat-only, Majority-2, Weighted-2, and Uniform-2 configurations. AUROC, TPR at δ≥{0.6, 1.0, 1.4}, FPR, and 5-fold CV variance.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The ensemble was the original Claim 2 pillar — a multi-signal detection system with broader coverage than any single method. In median tier, this vision collapses: DVD fails on MCQ, ConStat adds marginal signal, and the 2-method ensemble doesn't statistically outperform DIF alone. This is actually a clean narrative outcome — it supports the paper's pivot from \\\"detection system\\\" to \\\"diagnostic lens.\\\" DIF's value isn't as one component of a detection pipeline but as a psychometric characterization tool. The negative ensemble result reinforces the framing change. Brief appendix mention explaining why ensemble was attempted and why it doesn't add value.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, the ensemble degrades to a 2-signal combination after DVD exclusion. The weighted fusion AUROC (0.77) marginally exceeds DIF standalone (0.74) by +0.03, but the improvement CI [−0.02, 0.08] includes zero — not statistically significant. Majority voting (0.73) actually underperforms DIF alone because ConStat's lower precision drags down the consensus. Fusion weights (DIF 0.62, ConStat 0.38) heavily favor DIF, and the uniform-weight baseline (0.74) matches DIF standalone — confirming ConStat adds negligible complementary signal. Cross-validation variance (std=0.04) suggests the +0.03 improvement is within noise. The theoretical coverage argument (DIF for δ≥1.4, ConStat for non-RL δ≥0.6) partially holds, but the practical overlap in detectable items is too low (Jaccard=0.23) for fusion to exploit synergies — the methods disagree more than they complement. In median tier, this is a clear negative: ensemble doesn't justify added complexity. The paper's story is better served by presenting DIF as a standalone method with distinct properties rather than positioning it as part of a detection ensemble. Dropped from main body entirely.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cross_benchmark_gsm8k\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models from METABENCH). GSM1K contamination gap validation. Semi-synthetic injection on GSM8K. Item difficulty confound analysis.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"step1_gsm8k_temporal_dif\": {\n        \"n_items\": 1319,\n        \"n_models_total\": 6702,\n        \"n_ref_2023\": 1017,\n        \"n_foc_2024h1\": 804,\n        \"standard_c_pct\": 18.9,\n        \"external_c_pct\": 47.4,\n        \"sanity_c_pct\": 0.3,\n        \"standard_external_spearman\": 0.926\n      },\n      \"step2_semi_synthetic\": {\n        \"standard_delta_tpr_p05\": 0.612,\n        \"standard_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.818,\n        \"external_delta_tpr_p05\": 0.389,\n        \"external_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.517,\n        \"cross_subject_delta_fpr\": 0.0\n      },\n      \"step3_gsm1k_correlation\": {\n        \"n_models_matched\": 33,\n        \"n_dif_c_items\": 249,\n        \"pearson_r\": -0.478,\n        \"pearson_p\": 0.005,\n        \"pearson_bca_ci\": [\n          -0.714,\n          -0.155\n        ],\n        \"spearman_rho\": -0.403,\n        \"spearman_p\": 0.02,\n        \"spearman_bca_ci\": [\n          -0.687,\n          -0.031\n        ],\n        \"partial_r_controlling_accuracy\": -0.385,\n        \"partial_p_accuracy\": 0.027,\n        \"partial_r_controlling_accuracy_and_difficulty\": -0.289,\n        \"partial_p_accuracy_and_difficulty\": 0.108,\n        \"partial_r_bca_ci_acc_diff\": [\n          -0.551,\n          0.012\n        ],\n        \"partial_r_controlling_acc_diff_modelsize\": -0.251,\n        \"partial_p_acc_diff_modelsize\": 0.172,\n        \"partial_r_bca_ci_acc_diff_size\": [\n          -0.513,\n          0.048\n        ],\n        \"item_difficulty_dif_correlation\": -0.341,\n        \"item_difficulty_dif_p\": 0.003,\n        \"dif_c_mean_difficulty\": 0.412,\n        \"non_dif_mean_difficulty\": 0.587,\n        \"difficulty_confound_variance_explained\": 0.116,\n        \"permutation_p_raw\": 0.014,\n        \"permutation_p_partial_acc_diff\": 0.098,\n        \"cook_d_max_model\": \"Deepseek-math-7b-rl\",\n        \"cook_d_max_value\": 0.287,\n        \"leave_one_out_r_range\": [\n          -0.537,\n          -0.374\n        ],\n        \"dif_c_favor_2024\": 78,\n        \"dif_c_favor_2023\": 171\n      },\n      \"step3_median_tier_analysis\": {\n        \"degrees_of_freedom_3controls\": 28,\n        \"power_at_r025_n33\": 0.21,\n        \"power_at_r035_n33\": 0.38,\n        \"required_n_for_power80_r030\": 84\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: GSM8K temporal DIF and semi-synthetic validation\",\n        \"description\": \"Steps 1-2 results: C%, ΔTPR dose-response, ΔFPR. Confirms framework portability. Main body.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S7: GSM1K correlation sensitivity analysis\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows: Raw, +accuracy, +accuracy+difficulty, +accuracy+difficulty+size. Columns: partial r, p-value, BCa CI, permutation p. Shows progressive attenuation from −0.478 to −0.251. Supplementary (moved from main results).\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S8: Item difficulty confound characterization\",\n        \"description\": \"DIF-C vs non-DIF items: mean difficulty, difficulty distribution, correlation with DIF effect size. Shows DIF-C items are systematically harder.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The GSM1K correlation is the median tier's central vulnerability. The raw finding (r=−0.478) is real but not the whole story. Item difficulty is a plausible mediator OR confounder — DIF items are harder, harder items are more resistant to contamination-based gains, and harder items are more likely to show DIF because they have more variance in response patterns across model generations. This creates an identifiability problem: is the anti-correlation evidence that \\\"DIF captures genuine capability differences\\\" (the causal claim) or simply that \\\"harder items resist shallow memorization\\\" (a confound-driven association)? With N=33 and the difficulty confound, we cannot distinguish these hypotheses. The paper strategy: move GSM1K correlation from §4 main results to supplementary evidence, explicitly presenting the difficulty confound analysis. Downgrade the language from \\\"DIF items resist contamination-based gains\\\" to \\\"suggestive evidence consistent with DIF capturing genuine capability differences, partially confounded by item difficulty (N=33, exploratory).\\\" Claim 4 then rests on sub-findings 1-3 (redundancy, circularity ablation, diagnostic lens), which are self-contained on N=5,227 and don't depend on GSM1K. This is honest and defensible.\\n\\n\\nSteps 1-2 replicate cleanly: GSM8K temporal DIF (C%=18.9%, lower than MMLU's 31.3% due to single-domain homogeneity) and semi-synthetic framework (ΔTPR=+0.818) both confirm portability. Cross-subject matching continues to eliminate FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000). The median-tier weakness concentrates in Step 3: the raw GSM1K correlation (r=−0.478, p=0.005) survives basic scrutiny, but item difficulty is a genuine confound. DIF-C items are systematically harder (mean difficulty 0.412 vs 0.587 for non-DIF items, correlation −0.341, p=0.003), explaining 11.6% of variance in DIF status. Harder items naturally show larger contamination gaps because contamination-based gains concentrate on items models would otherwise get wrong — and harder items have more room for this. Controlling for both accuracy AND item difficulty drops partial r from −0.478 to −0.289 (p=0.108), with BCa CI [−0.551, 0.012] now including zero at the upper bound. Adding model size as a third control further attenuates to r=−0.251 (p=0.172). With N=33 and 3 controls, df≈28, and power to detect r=0.30 is only 0.38 — the test is underpowered to distinguish a real moderate effect from zero. Permutation p for the partial correlation is 0.098 — suggestive but not significant at α=0.05. Leave-one-out analysis shows robustness to individual influential models (range [−0.537, −0.374] for raw r), but this doesn't address the difficulty confound. The point estimate (r≈−0.29) still suggests DIF items resist contamination-based gains, but the evidence is now \\\"suggestive and exploratory\\\" rather than \\\"confirmed.\\\" N=84 would be needed for 80% power at this effect size — achievable if GSM1K coverage expands.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\",\n    \"description\": \"Pilot bifactor IRT model on MMLU for anchor item selection and equated score calibration. Test whether bifactor specification addresses unidimensional IRT misfit. Fixed-parameter calibration of replacement items.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"bifactor_convergence_full\": false,\n      \"bifactor_convergence_partial\": \"32/57 subjects converge\",\n      \"converged_subjects_mean_n_items\": 247,\n      \"failed_subjects_mean_n_items\": 84,\n      \"general_factor_variance_explained\": 0.41,\n      \"subject_factor_avg_variance\": 0.13,\n      \"subject_factor_max_variance\": 0.29,\n      \"subject_factor_min_variance\": 0.02,\n      \"rmsea_bifactor_converged\": 0.038,\n      \"rmsea_unidimensional\": 0.041,\n      \"cfi_bifactor_converged\": 0.87,\n      \"cfi_unidimensional\": 0.82,\n      \"cfi_target\": 0.9,\n      \"anchor_items_selected\": 1847,\n      \"anchor_items_dif_free_pct\": 92.3,\n      \"anchor_items_per_subject_mean\": 57.7,\n      \"equated_rho_5pct_replace\": 0.982,\n      \"equated_rho_10pct_replace\": 0.964,\n      \"equated_rho_20pct_replace\": 0.937,\n      \"equated_rho_31pct_replace\": 0.908,\n      \"equated_rho_target\": 0.95,\n      \"target_met_at_pct\": 14,\n      \"calibration_rmse_difficulty\": 0.31,\n      \"calibration_rmse_discrimination\": 0.28,\n      \"computation_hours\": 38\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S9: Bifactor IRT fit comparison\",\n        \"description\": \"Unidimensional vs bifactor fit indices (RMSEA, CFI, TLI, SRMR) on converged subjects. Shows improvement but still below standard benchmarks.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S10: Equating quality by replacement rate\",\n        \"description\": \"Replacement rate (5/10/14/20/31%) × Spearman ρ, Kendall τ, top-10 overlap, calibration RMSE. Critical threshold highlighted at 14%.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The bifactor pilot confirms that unidimensional IRT misfit on MMLU is structural (57 subjects) and partially addressable but not solvable with bifactor specification. This result serves two purposes in median tier: (1) it honestly demonstrates the IRT limitation and the concrete failure mode (partial convergence, below-target CFI), providing material for the expanded §5 limitations section that a reviewer will demand; (2) it motivates the specific \\\"multidimensional IRT with domain-specific factors\\\" future work recommendation with empirical evidence (bifactor's partial success shows the direction is right, just needs more flexible specification). The equating quality curve provides practical guidance: benchmark developers can safely replace up to ~14% of items while maintaining ρ≥0.95. Appendix material.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, the bifactor model partially addresses IRT misfit but doesn't fully solve it. Full convergence fails because 25/57 MMLU subjects have insufficient items (mean 84) or near-zero subject-specific variance for stable bifactor estimation. The converged subset shows improved fit (CFI 0.82→0.87, RMSEA 0.041→0.038), but CFI remains below the 0.90 benchmark — MMLU's 57-subject structure is too complex for bifactor (which assumes a single general factor plus subject-specific factors; MMLU likely needs a hierarchical structure with domain clusters). Anchor item selection works well within converged subjects: 1,847 items with 92.3% DIF-free status, providing a stable calibration foundation. However, equating quality degrades sharply with replacement rate: ρ=0.982 at 5% but only 0.908 at 31% (the full DIF-C proportion), falling below the 0.95 target at ~14% replacement. Calibration RMSE of 0.31 for difficulty means replacement items are only approximately matched — each replacement introduces ±0.31 logits of difficulty error, which compounds at high replacement rates. In median tier, this is an honest negative pilot: the approach works for small-scale replacement (≤14%) but doesn't scale to the full DIF remediation needed. Demoted to appendix as methodological pilot, with the IRT misfit result feeding into the §5 limitations expansion. The partial convergence is actually useful evidence for the \\\"future multidimensional IRT work\\\" recommendation.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"item_generation_calibration\",\n    \"description\": \"Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions using LLM generation with construct constraints. Calibrate via student simulation. Compare difficulty prediction approaches.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_generated\": 400,\n      \"n_subjects_covered\": 28,\n      \"n_items_passing_quality_filter\": 312,\n      \"quality_pass_rate\": 0.78,\n      \"construct_alignment_human_eval\": 0.81,\n      \"construct_alignment_n_raters\": 3,\n      \"construct_alignment_krippendorf_alpha\": 0.68,\n      \"cognitive_level_match_rate\": 0.73,\n      \"difficulty_rho_prompt_based\": 0.32,\n      \"difficulty_rho_student_sim\": 0.58,\n      \"difficulty_rho_student_sim_ci\": [\n        0.49,\n        0.66\n      ],\n      \"difficulty_rho_target\": 0.75,\n      \"difficulty_rmse_prompt\": 0.51,\n      \"difficulty_rmse_student_sim\": 0.34,\n      \"holdout_panel_n_models\": 50,\n      \"holdout_panel_correlation\": 0.61,\n      \"discrimination_mean_generated\": 0.87,\n      \"discrimination_mean_original\": 1.14,\n      \"discrimination_gap\": -0.27,\n      \"distractor_quality_mean\": 0.62,\n      \"distractor_quality_original\": 0.71,\n      \"generation_model\": \"GPT-4-turbo\",\n      \"cost_per_item_usd\": 0.42\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S11: Item generation quality metrics\",\n        \"description\": \"Overall and by-subject quality pass rate, construct alignment, difficulty correlation (prompt vs student-sim vs holdout), discrimination comparison.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Item generation calibration is the second pillar of the regeneration claim (original Claim 3), and its below-target performance confirms the regeneration pathway is premature. The difficulty calibration gap (ρ=0.58 vs target 0.75) is the binding constraint — even with perfect equating (plan_005), imprecise replacement items would degrade benchmark validity. The discrimination gap (−0.27) is equally concerning: lower-discrimination items provide less measurement information per item, meaning a regenerated benchmark would need MORE items to achieve equivalent precision. In median tier, this negative result supports the paper's pivot from \\\"regeneration\\\" to \\\"diagnostic lens\\\" — DIF's value is in characterizing what changed, not in enabling automated item replacement. One paragraph in appendix, framed as evidence that \\\"automated benchmark maintenance requires psychometric calibration advances beyond current LLM generation capability.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, item generation achieves mechanical success but psychometric inadequacy. Quality pass rate (78%) and construct alignment (0.81) indicate generated items are topically appropriate, but difficulty calibration is the bottleneck. Prompt-based targeting (ρ=0.32) is consistent with published estimates (0.10-0.45); student simulation improves to ρ=0.58 but falls short of the 0.75-0.82 benchmark from the referenced paper. The gap likely reflects MMLU's domain breadth — student simulation models are trained on specific domains and don't transfer well across MMLU's 57 subjects. Generated items have lower discrimination (0.87 vs 1.14 original), meaning they separate high- and low-ability models less effectively. Distractor quality (0.62 vs 0.71) is also lower — generated distractors attract fewer models, making items easier to guess. The holdout panel correlation (0.61) confirms difficulty mismatch isn't just calibration error but reflects genuine behavioral differences between generated and original items. Inter-rater reliability (Krippendorff's α=0.68) is acceptable but not strong, introducing human evaluation noise. In median tier, this is a below-benchmark result: the pipeline works but doesn't produce items with sufficient psychometric properties for valid equating. Combined with plan_005's equating quality degradation, the regeneration pathway is not viable with current technology. Brief appendix mention as negative pilot.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cumulative_bayesian_dif\",\n    \"description\": \"Simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006) across 4 successive benchmark administrations. Characterize power accumulation for medium DIF effects.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_administrations\": 4,\n      \"n_items_per_admin\": 3000,\n      \"n_models_per_admin\": 500,\n      \"effect_size_delta\": 1.0,\n      \"single_admin_power\": 0.41,\n      \"cumulative_2admin_power\": 0.53,\n      \"cumulative_3admin_power\": 0.62,\n      \"cumulative_4admin_power\": 0.68,\n      \"target_3admin_power\": 0.7,\n      \"target_met\": false,\n      \"power_gain_per_admin\": [\n        0.12,\n        0.09,\n        0.06\n      ],\n      \"prior_flat_power_4admin\": 0.65,\n      \"prior_informative_power_4admin\": 0.68,\n      \"prior_sensitivity_delta\": 0.03,\n      \"fpr_single\": 0.048,\n      \"fpr_cumulative_4\": 0.052,\n      \"fpr_inflation\": 0.004,\n      \"bayes_factor_threshold\": 3.0,\n      \"pct_items_bf_above_3_single\": 0.28,\n      \"pct_items_bf_above_3_cumulative4\": 0.51,\n      \"computation_time_minutes\": 14,\n      \"convergence_diagnostics_rhat_max\": 1.03\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S12: Cumulative Bayesian DIF power across administrations\",\n        \"description\": \"Rows: 1-4 administrations. Columns: power (flat prior), power (informative prior), FPR, Bayes factor >3 proportion, cumulative data points.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cumulative Bayesian DIF is theoretically appealing for longitudinal benchmark monitoring but empirically modest in median tier. The power gains are real but slow — 4 administrations are needed to approach what a single larger-cohort MH analysis achieves at δ≥1.4. The practical implication is that temporal accumulation supplements rather than replaces cross-sectional DIF. In median tier, this goes to appendix with framing: \\\"Bayesian evidence accumulation is a promising direction for detecting gradual item degradation, but current power gains are incremental. Practical deployment requires automated pipeline integration with leaderboard update cycles.\\\" This connects to the \\\"actionable recommendation\\\" the narrative guidance demands.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, cumulative Bayesian DIF shows the expected monotonic power improvement but falls short of targets. Power rises from 0.41 (single administration) to 0.68 (4 administrations) for medium effects (δ=1.0) — below the 0.70 target at 3 administrations (0.62). The shortfall reflects realistic MMLU item parameters (a_mean=1.2, lower discrimination than textbook examples) and the relatively small cohort sizes per administration (N=500). Diminishing returns are evident: +0.12 from admin 1→2, +0.09 from 2→3, +0.06 from 3→4 — each additional administration provides less lift as the posterior narrows. Prior sensitivity is minimal (flat vs informative: Δ=0.03), suggesting the data dominates the prior even after 4 administrations — a positive robustness finding but also indicating the priors aren't adding much value. FPR inflation is negligible (+0.004 over 4 administrations), confirming the Bayesian framework controls Type I error well. The simulation is internally clean but has two fundamental limitations: (1) it's simulation-only — no real multi-administration LLM benchmark data exists to validate against; (2) it assumes stationary DIF patterns across administrations, which is unlikely in the rapidly evolving LLM landscape. In median tier, this is a correct but incremental simulation study that belongs in the appendix as motivation for longitudinal DIF monitoring, not a main contribution.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"graceful_degradation_analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement. Validity metrics (ranking stability, score reliability, construct coverage) as function of replacement rate.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"replacement_rates_tested\": [\n        0.05,\n        0.1,\n        0.15,\n        0.2,\n        0.25,\n        0.3,\n        0.5\n      ],\n      \"rho_at_5pct\": 0.994,\n      \"rho_at_10pct\": 0.987,\n      \"rho_at_15pct\": 0.976,\n      \"rho_at_20pct\": 0.971,\n      \"rho_at_25pct\": 0.958,\n      \"rho_at_30pct\": 0.948,\n      \"rho_at_50pct\": 0.923,\n      \"reliability_at_5pct\": 0.989,\n      \"reliability_at_10pct\": 0.981,\n      \"reliability_at_15pct\": 0.972,\n      \"reliability_at_20pct\": 0.964,\n      \"reliability_at_25pct\": 0.951,\n      \"reliability_at_30pct\": 0.94,\n      \"reliability_at_50pct\": 0.912,\n      \"construct_coverage_at_5pct\": 0.97,\n      \"construct_coverage_at_10pct\": 0.94,\n      \"construct_coverage_at_20pct\": 0.89,\n      \"construct_coverage_at_30pct\": 0.83,\n      \"construct_coverage_at_50pct\": 0.72,\n      \"critical_threshold_rho_095\": 0.27,\n      \"critical_threshold_reliability_095\": 0.24,\n      \"critical_threshold_coverage_090\": 0.18,\n      \"degradation_linear_fit_rho_r2\": 0.96,\n      \"degradation_linear_fit_reliability_r2\": 0.98,\n      \"random_vs_dif_targeted_replacement\": {\n        \"random_30pct_rho\": 0.948,\n        \"dif_targeted_30pct_rho\": 0.952,\n        \"difference\": 0.004\n      },\n      \"n_bootstrap_iterations\": 1000\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S13: Validity metrics under progressive replacement\",\n        \"description\": \"Replacement rate × ranking stability (ρ), score reliability (α), construct coverage. Critical thresholds highlighted. Random vs DIF-targeted comparison at 30%.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Graceful degradation quantifies the practical limits of item replacement for MMLU benchmark maintenance. The 27% critical threshold (ρ<0.95) provides a concrete operational bound that benchmark developers can use. The finding that DIF-targeted and random replacement produce similar degradation reinforces plan_017/020's conclusion that DIF items are not the most \\\"important\\\" items — they're items whose functioning changed, not items that carry the most ranking information. In median tier, this is clean supplementary material that supports the \\\"instability ≠ invalidity\\\" framing with quantitative bounds. Appendix.\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, graceful degradation shows clean, near-linear validity loss under progressive replacement. The key finding: ρ drops below 0.95 at ~27% replacement rate, just below the 31% DIF-C item proportion. This means replacing ALL DIF items would marginally violate the 0.95 stability threshold (ρ=0.948 at 30%). However, this uses random replacement items (not calibrated), so it measures a worst-case scenario. Construct coverage degrades faster than ranking stability (drops below 0.90 at 18%) because randomly replacing items within a subject can eliminate coverage of specific cognitive skills within that domain. The DIF-targeted vs random replacement comparison (0.952 vs 0.948 at 30%) shows negligible difference — DIF items are not preferentially \\\"important\\\" for ranking, which is consistent with plan_017's finding that DIF removal is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy. In median tier, this provides useful quantitative context for the \\\"instability ≠ invalidity\\\" framing: it shows that MMLU can tolerate substantial item replacement without catastrophic validity loss. The 27% critical threshold is close enough to 31% that it adds narrative tension — DIF items are at the boundary of safe replacement. Clean supplementary analysis.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"item_focused_trees\",\n    \"description\": \"Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) using model release date as continuous covariate. Compare DIF detection with continuous vs categorical (MH) approach. Nonlinear DIF trend detection.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_analyzed\": 12508,\n      \"ift_significant_items\": 4102,\n      \"mh_significant_items_ets_c\": 3918,\n      \"overlap_items\": 3064,\n      \"ift_unique_items\": 1038,\n      \"mh_unique_items\": 854,\n      \"overlap_jaccard\": 0.74,\n      \"release_date_split_point_modal\": \"2023-09\",\n      \"release_date_split_point_range\": [\n        \"2023-06\",\n        \"2024-01\"\n      ],\n      \"nonlinear_trend_items_pct\": 8.3,\n      \"nonlinear_trend_items_n\": 1038,\n      \"monotonic_increasing_dif_pct\": 52.1,\n      \"monotonic_decreasing_dif_pct\": 39.6,\n      \"u_shaped_dif_pct\": 5.1,\n      \"inverted_u_dif_pct\": 3.2,\n      \"tree_depth_median\": 2,\n      \"tree_depth_max\": 4,\n      \"model_convergence_rate\": 0.94,\n      \"computation_hours\": 3.2,\n      \"ift_effect_size_correlation_with_mh\": 0.81,\n      \"ift_unique_items_mean_delta_mh\": 1.12\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S14: IFT vs MH DIF detection comparison\",\n        \"description\": \"Cross-tabulation of IFT-flagged vs MH-flagged items. Overlap, unique-to-each, Jaccard. Mean DIF effect size in each cell. IFT-unique items characterized by gradual trend.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S15: Non-linear DIF trend typology\",\n        \"description\": \"DIF trend types (monotonic increasing/decreasing, U-shaped, inverted-U) with prevalence, example subjects, and interpretation. 8.3% non-linear.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"IFT confirms MH's core finding from a different methodological angle: MMLU items exhibit substantial temporal DIF regardless of whether cohorts are defined categorically or continuously. The Jaccard of 0.74 is high enough to validate MH but low enough to show MH misses some gradual-onset DIF items. The nonlinear trends (8.3%) are genuinely interesting — they suggest some items' functioning is non-monotonic over model generations, possibly reflecting specific model family effects that come and go. However, this is supplementary nuance, not a main finding. In median tier, IFT goes to appendix as robustness check supporting the statement: \\\"Temporal DIF detection is robust to cohort definition methodology; continuous approaches capture additional gradual-onset items but do not qualitatively change the instability finding.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, Item-Focused Trees detect 4,102 items with significant DIF using continuous release date, compared to 3,918 from categorical MH — a Jaccard of 0.74 shows substantial overlap. The 1,038 IFT-unique items have mean Δ_MH=1.12, below the ETS C threshold (|Δ|≥1.5) but identified by IFT because their DIF trend is gradual and monotonic — individually they're ETS B (moderate) but collectively they represent consistent temporal drift. The 854 MH-unique items tend to have abrupt DIF patterns at the temporal boundary that IFT's recursive partitioning smooths over. The modal split point (2023-09) is earlier than MH's categorical boundary (2024-01), suggesting the onset of DIF for many items predates the arbitrary cohort split. 8.3% of items show nonlinear DIF trends (U-shaped or inverted-U), which neither MH nor linear IFT captures well — these are items that functioned differently in mid-2023 models but reverted by 2024, possibly reflecting transient training data or architecture trends. The effect size correlation (0.81 with MH) confirms both methods capture the same underlying signal with different sensitivity profiles. In median tier, IFT provides useful robustness evidence that the core DIF finding is not an artifact of arbitrary cohort boundaries. The nonlinear trends add nuance but are too rare (8.3%) to qualitatively change the story. Appendix robustness check.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"architecture_stratified_dif\",\n    \"description\": \"Run DIF within architecture-homogeneous subpopulation (decoder-only only) to test whether IRT fit improves and whether architecture heterogeneity contributes to spurious DIF.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_models\": 5227,\n      \"decoder_only_models\": 5226,\n      \"non_decoder_models\": 1,\n      \"encoder_decoder_models\": 0,\n      \"decoder_only_pct\": 99.98,\n      \"decoder_only_temporal_c_pct\": 31.3,\n      \"full_population_temporal_c_pct\": 31.3,\n      \"jaccard_decoder_vs_full\": 1.0,\n      \"delta_mh_correlation_decoder_vs_full\": 0.9999,\n      \"rmsea_decoder_only\": 0.041,\n      \"rmsea_full\": 0.041,\n      \"cfi_decoder_only\": 0.82,\n      \"cfi_full\": 0.82,\n      \"tli_decoder_only\": 0.8,\n      \"tli_full\": 0.8,\n      \"srmr_decoder_only\": 0.048,\n      \"srmr_full\": 0.048,\n      \"irt_lz_pct_above_2_decoder\": 98.1,\n      \"irt_lz_pct_above_2_full\": 98.2,\n      \"architecture_confound_effect\": \"none (single non-decoder model)\",\n      \"single_non_decoder_model\": \"flan-t5-xl or similar\",\n      \"c_pct_change_removing_non_decoder\": 0.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Architecture composition and DIF robustness\",\n        \"description\": \"Architecture breakdown (decoder-only: 5226, other: 1). DIF statistics: C%, Jaccard, IRT fit indices for full vs decoder-only populations. All identical.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Architecture stratification dissolves a potential reviewer concern with minimal effort. The 99.98% decoder-only composition means architecture heterogeneity is empirically irrelevant for METABENCH MMLU. This should be presented proactively in §4.1 (immediately after the main DIF result) to prevent the reviewer from even forming the objection. The persistent IRT misfit (CFI=0.82) after removing the single non-decoder model is used as evidence that misfit is structural (multidimensionality) rather than compositional (architecture), supporting the §5 limitations expansion. In median tier, this is a clean 1-paragraph robustness check in the main body: \\\"Architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue: METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5,226/5,227); excluding the single non-decoder model produces identical DIF results (Jaccard=1.0). IRT misfit persists (CFI=0.82), confirming it reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality, not architecture mixing (see §5).\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn median tier, architecture stratification is a complete non-issue — and this is itself a useful finding. METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models), with a single encoder-decoder model (likely a Flan-T5 variant or similar). Removing this one model changes literally nothing: Jaccard=1.0, C% identical to 4 decimal places, Δ_MH correlation=0.9999. IRT fit diagnostics (RMSEA=0.041, CFI=0.82, |Lz|>2 for 98.1% of models) are identical in the decoder-only subset because the fit problem is structural (57-subject multidimensionality), not compositional (architecture mixing). This result serves two purposes: (1) it completely preempts the reviewer concern \\\"maybe DIF is detecting architecture differences, not temporal changes\\\" — the population IS architecture-homogeneous; (2) it honestly demonstrates that IRT misfit persists even in a homogeneous population, confirming the misfit is inherent to applying unidimensional IRT to multidimensional MMLU. The first purpose is a defense; the second is an honest limitation that feeds into the §5 expansion. Main body robustness check (1-2 paragraphs in §4.1).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Inject known contamination into METABENCH response matrix and measure DIF detection power under controlled ground truth. Cross-subject vs within-subject matching comparison. Dose-response characterization.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_clean_items_selected\": 500,\n      \"n_injected_items\": 150,\n      \"injection_rates_tested\": [\n        0.0,\n        0.3,\n        0.5,\n        0.7,\n        1.0\n      ],\n      \"temporal_cohort_standard_matching\": {\n        \"baseline_fpr\": 0.298,\n        \"tpr_p00\": 0.298,\n        \"tpr_p03\": 0.483,\n        \"tpr_p05\": 0.596,\n        \"tpr_p07\": 0.812,\n        \"tpr_p10\": 0.972,\n        \"delta_tpr_p03\": 0.185,\n        \"delta_tpr_p05\": 0.298,\n        \"delta_tpr_p07\": 0.514,\n        \"delta_tpr_p10\": 0.674,\n        \"fpr_inflation_p05\": 0.147,\n        \"fpr_inflation_p10\": 0.318\n      },\n      \"temporal_cohort_external_matching\": {\n        \"baseline_fpr\": 0.314,\n        \"tpr_p00\": 0.326,\n        \"tpr_p03\": 0.548,\n        \"tpr_p05\": 0.834,\n        \"tpr_p07\": 0.99,\n        \"tpr_p10\": 1.0,\n        \"delta_tpr_p03\": 0.222,\n        \"delta_tpr_p05\": 0.508,\n        \"delta_tpr_p07\": 0.664,\n        \"delta_tpr_p10\": 0.674,\n        \"delta_fpr\": 0.0,\n        \"fpr_stable\": true\n      },\n      \"within_subject_matching_fpr_inflation\": 0.186,\n      \"cross_subject_matching_delta_fpr\": 0.0,\n      \"dose_response_r2\": 0.97,\n      \"dose_response_slope\": 0.68,\n      \"ability_cohort_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.302,\n      \"matching_contamination_confirmed\": true,\n      \"inf_delta_bug_fixed\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: Semi-synthetic injection detection power\",\n        \"description\": \"Injection rate (p=0.0 to 1.0) × matching strategy (standard, external/cross-subject, within-subject). TPR, ΔTPR, FPR, ΔFPR. Highlights cross-subject ΔFPR=0.000.\\n\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table S16: Matching variable contamination analysis\",\n        \"description\": \"Detailed FPR breakdown by matching strategy and injection rate. Shows within-subject FPR inflation mechanism and cross-subject immunity.\\n\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Semi-synthetic injection is the strongest methodological contribution in median tier. It solves the ground truth circularity problem (Li et al. proxy labels can't validate item-level differential detectors) by injecting known differential contamination into real response matrices. The cross-subject matching insight is a practical innovation with immediate applicability: any researcher applying MH-DIF to multi-domain benchmarks should use cross-domain scores as matching variables. The ΔFPR=0.000 result is unusually clean — it's not \\\"small FPR inflation\\\" but literally zero across all injection rates and all 5 tested subjects. This robustness makes it a credible methodological recommendation. The dose-response curve provides the operating characteristics a practitioner needs: at p=0.5 exploitation rate, cross-subject DIF achieves ΔTPR≈0.50 — meaningful detection power that complements the raw ΔTPR with calibrated ground truth. In median tier, this is the contribution most likely to survive reviewer scrutiny because it's self-contained, internally validated, and immediately useful. §4 main body, Claim 3.\\n\\nIn median tier, semi-synthetic injection is the cleanest methodological contribution. ΔTPR at p=0.5 is +0.508 (external matching), demonstrating DIF has genuine detection power for differential contamination when ground truth is controlled. The dose-response (R²=0.97) follows a near-linear trajectory from p=0.3 to p=1.0, confirming the relationship between contamination intensity and detection power is well-behaved. The critical methodological insight is cross-subject matching: when the MH matching variable (total score) is computed on OTHER subjects, FPR is completely stable (ΔFPR=0.000) across all injection rates. Within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable because injected correct answers inflate the total score on the same items being tested, creating a circular dependency (FPR inflation +0.186). This cross-subject insight is non-obvious — it requires understanding that MH conditions on total score, and that contamination inflates total score on affected items, creating artificial ability group shifts. The insight generalizes to any multi-domain benchmark where cross-domain scores can serve as matching variables. Standard matching (total score on ALL items including tested ones) shows intermediate behavior — some FPR inflation (+0.318 at p=1.0) but less than within-subject because contamination signal is diluted across 57 subjects. In median tier, this is a main body result (§4, Claim 3) with the cross-subject matching as the primary methodological recommendation.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":618,"startLine":1,"totalLines":618}}}
