{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SBMKBuP5j4FT6gxk77RDb2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MBdQgSDUZKooUfpv3XmA7e","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T042647/median/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\title{Temporal Differential Item Functioning Reveals 31\\% Measurement Instability in MMLU:\\\\A Psychometric Audit of 5{,}227 Language Models}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%=============================================================================\n% ABSTRACT\n%=============================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nWhen we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores measure the same construct.\nWe test this assumption by applying Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis---a classical psychometric method for detecting items whose difficulty shifts across examinee groups after controlling for ability---to the METABENCH response matrix (5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items).\nWe find that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category~C) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts.\nThis instability is not a statistical artifact (random-split sanity: 0.18\\%), is not driven by domain-level capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\% of DIF explained), and does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$ after removing all unstable items).\nWe show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$) and provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false-positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000).\nDIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal but are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy, serving as a diagnostic lens for understanding which capability dimensions shift across model generations.\nWe recommend benchmark developers run temporal DIF analysis before each leaderboard update cycle.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%=============================================================================\n% 1. INTRODUCTION\n%=============================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nLarge Language Model (LLM) benchmarks serve as the primary instrument for comparing model capabilities and tracking progress.\nMMLU~\\citep{hendrycks2021measuring}, with its 57 subjects spanning STEM, humanities, and social sciences, has become a de facto standard for general-purpose evaluation.\nLeaderboards rank thousands of models on MMLU scores, and the resulting orderings shape research priorities, funding decisions, and deployment choices.\n\nThese rankings implicitly assume \\emph{temporal score comparability}: a score of 70\\% in 2023 and 70\\% in 2024 should reflect the same level of capability.\nBut benchmark items exist in an evolving ecosystem---training data expands, model architectures change, and the boundary between memorization and generalization shifts.\nIf specific items become systematically easier or harder for newer models \\emph{beyond what overall ability differences predict}, then cross-temporal comparisons on those items conflate measurement with measurement error.\n\nPsychometrics offers a mature framework for testing exactly this assumption: Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis.\nDIF detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups \\emph{after controlling for overall ability}---separating genuine item instability from the expected consequence of ``models got better.''\nThe Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure, standardized since 1988 with the ETS classification system, has been applied millions of times in educational testing but never at the scale of modern LLM benchmarks.\n\nA critical framing point: \\textbf{instability does not imply invalidity.}\nDIF flags items whose functioning changed across model generations---this is a \\emph{diagnostic signal}, not a quality verdict.\nIf MMLU rankings are robust to removing all unstable items, then instability reveals which capability dimensions are shifting without threatening the benchmark's overall measurement validity.\nThis distinction---between item-level instability and benchmark-level validity---must guide interpretation of our findings.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items), constituting the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date. Our contributions:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Temporal instability characterization.} 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF (ETS~C, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 cohorts, robust across thresholds (10--49\\%), multiple-testing correction, and architecture composition (99.98\\% decoder-only).\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique.} Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Any item-level detector validated against these labels has a fundamental blind spot. We provide the first empirical demonstration (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$).\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.} A $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) that provides controlled ground truth for any item-level contamination detector. Portable to GSM8K ($\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818).\n\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens.} DIF items carry cross-cohort signal ($z=-8$ to $-49\\sigma$ vs.\\ random removal) but are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy ($z=-9.5$ vs.\\ accuracy-gap $z=-157$). DIF isolates genuine differential functioning, not just informative items.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%=============================================================================\n% 2. RELATED WORK\n%=============================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nSeveral lines of work address data contamination in LLM evaluation.\nPerturbation-based methods such as ConStat test whether models are sensitive to semantically equivalent reformulations of benchmark items, achieving moderate detection rates on non-RL models but degrading substantially on models trained with reinforcement learning.\nMemorization-based approaches (Min-K\\%, n-gram overlap) detect verbatim memorization signatures but miss non-verbatim contamination modes.\nGeneration-variance methods (DVD) analyze response distributions under temperature sampling, designed for open-ended generation rather than multiple-choice formats.\nAll these approaches share a common validation gap: they evaluate against web-overlap or n-gram ground truth labels that measure whether an item is \\emph{publicly accessible}, not whether it \\emph{functions differently} across model cohorts.\nOur DIF-ConStat overlap analysis (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.23; Appendix~\\ref{app:constat}) confirms that perturbation methods and DIF capture largely orthogonal contamination dimensions---DIF detects items with differential difficulty across cohorts conditioning on ability, while perturbation methods detect surface-feature exploitation.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometrics in NLP evaluation.}\nItem Response Theory (IRT) has been applied to NLP benchmarks for item difficulty estimation, adaptive testing, and model ability measurement.\nHowever, IRT-based \\emph{DIF analysis}---which requires comparing item parameters across groups---has not been applied to LLM benchmarks at scale.\nOur work bridges this gap, bringing the MH-DIF framework from educational testing to LLM evaluation.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark validity and temporal dynamics.}\nRecent work has raised concerns about benchmark saturation, contamination, and the meaningful interpretation of aggregate scores.\n``Growing Pains'' examines anchor-item strategies for efficient cross-temporal evaluation.\nOur DIF analysis complements this by identifying \\emph{which} anchor items are degrading---a diagnostic that anchor-item approaches require but do not themselves provide.\n\n%=============================================================================\n% 3. METHOD\n%=============================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF Analysis}\n\\label{sec:mh-dif}\n\nDifferential Item Functioning (DIF) occurs when examinees of equal ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering an item correctly.\nThe Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure tests for \\emph{uniform} DIF---a constant difference in item difficulty across ability levels---by constructing $2 \\times 2$ contingency tables at each ability stratum and computing a common odds ratio.\n\nFormally, for item $j$, let models be divided into a reference group $R$ (e.g., 2023 models) and a focal group $F$ (e.g., 2024-H1 models), stratified into $K$ ability levels based on total score. At stratum $k$, we observe:\n\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_\\text{MH} = \\frac{\\sum_{k=1}^{K} A_k D_k / N_k}{\\sum_{k=1}^{K} B_k C_k / N_k}\n\\end{equation}\n\n\\noindent where $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are the cell counts of the $2 \\times 2$ table (correct/incorrect $\\times$ reference/focal) and $N_k$ is the stratum total. The MH delta statistic is:\n\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta_\\text{MH} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_\\text{MH})\n\\end{equation}\n\nThe Educational Testing Service (ETS) classifies items by DIF severity: Category~A (negligible, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.0$), Category~B (moderate, $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.5$), and Category~C (large, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$ and statistically significant).\n\n\\paragraph{Cohort definitions.}\nWe define two primary cohorts: a \\emph{temporal} cohort splitting models by release date (2023 reference, $N_R = 1{,}253$; 2024-H1 focal, $N_F = 656$), and an \\emph{ability} cohort splitting by performance quintile. The temporal cohort tests whether item functioning shifts across model generations; the ability cohort serves as a control (DIF should be low if the method is not detecting trivial difficulty differences).\n\n\\paragraph{Limitation: non-uniform DIF.}\nMH detects only \\emph{uniform} DIF---cases where the item favors one group consistently across all ability levels.\nNon-uniform DIF, where the direction of advantage reverses at different ability levels (crossing ICCs), requires methods like SIBTEST or logistic regression DIF.\nWe address this limitation by running SIBTEST as a concordance check.\nSIBTEST identifies an additional 11.2\\% of items (1{,}406) with significant non-uniform DIF beyond MH's flags, yielding a total temporal instability estimate of ${\\sim}42.5\\%$.\nThe MH-SIBTEST Jaccard concordance of 0.71 on uniform DIF confirms that MH captures the dominant signal: of the items flagged by either method for uniform DIF, 71\\% are flagged by both.\nCrossing-ICC items (where the item favors older models at low ability but newer models at high ability) constitute 7.8\\% of all items.\nWe present MH as the primary analysis because it is the most widely validated and interpretable DIF procedure, while acknowledging that total instability exceeds the 31.3\\% uniform-DIF estimate.\nThe SIBTEST concordance provides evidence that uniform DIF captures the dominant mode of item instability in this population.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Injection Framework}\n\\label{sec:semi-synthetic}\n\nExisting contamination detection methods are validated against web-overlap or n-gram labels that measure \\emph{global exposure}---whether an item appeared in training data---not \\emph{differential contamination}---whether an item functions differently across model cohorts.\nWe propose a controlled validation framework.\n\n\\paragraph{Injection procedure.}\nStarting from a clean subset of the real METABENCH response matrix ($N_\\text{clean} = 500$ items), we inject known differential contamination by flipping incorrect$\\to$correct responses for a random subset of focal-group models at rate $p \\in \\{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$ on $N_\\text{inject} = 150$ target items.\nThis preserves the real ability distribution and item characteristics while providing ground-truth contaminated items.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}\nA subtle but critical methodological point: the MH procedure conditions on total score as a proxy for ability.\nWhen contamination inflates correct responses on tested items, the total score computed on \\emph{those same items} is also inflated, creating a circular dependency that inflates false-positive rates.\nWe resolve this with \\emph{cross-subject matching}: computing the matching variable (total score) on subjects \\emph{other than} the one being tested.\nThis eliminates matching-variable contamination entirely ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 across all injection rates and all tested subjects), while within-subject matching suffers FPR inflation of +0.186.\n\n\\paragraph{Evaluation metrics.}\nWe report $\\Delta$TPR (true-positive rate increase from baseline at each injection rate) and $\\Delta$FPR (false-positive rate change) to characterize detection power independently of the baseline DIF rate.\n\n%=============================================================================\n% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n%=============================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF Prevalence on MMLU (Claim 1)}\n\\label{sec:temporal-dif}\n\n\\paragraph{Setup.}\nWe apply MH-DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 items) with the temporal cohort definition (2023 reference, $N=1{,}253$; 2024-H1 focal, $N=656$).\n\n\\paragraph{Results.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:dif-prevalence} summarizes DIF prevalence.\n31.3\\% of items ($N=3{,}918$) are classified as ETS Category~C (large DIF), 20.1\\% as Category~B, and 48.6\\% as Category~A.\nThe ability-quintile cohort yields only 2.9\\% Category~C items, and the Jaccard overlap between temporal and ability DIF flags is 0.014---confirming the signal is temporal, not a trivial consequence of ability differences.\nA random-split sanity check produces 0.18\\% Category~C, establishing the empirical false-positive floor.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF prevalence across cohort definitions and item filtering. ETS categories: A (negligible), B (moderate), C (large). SIBTEST non-uniform counts shown for temporal cohort.}\n\\label{tab:dif-prevalence}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort} & \\textbf{$N_\\text{ref}$} & \\textbf{$N_\\text{foc}$} & \\textbf{A\\%} & \\textbf{B\\%} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{SIBTEST add'l\\%} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal & 1{,}253 & 656 & 48.6 & 20.1 & 31.3 & +11.2 \\\\\nTemporal (no neg-disc) & 1{,}253 & 656 & --- & --- & 29.8 & --- \\\\\nAbility quintile & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{quintiles} & --- & --- & 2.9 & --- \\\\\nRandom split (sanity) & --- & --- & --- & --- & 0.18 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Domain-specificity analysis.}\nA natural concern is that DIF simply reflects ``models got better at certain subjects.''\nSubject-level accuracy improvement correlates modestly with DIF prevalence (Spearman $\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$), but item-level decomposition shows domain-level improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\\% C-rate (7.6\\%; pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$).\nThe instability is predominantly item-specific.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture robustness.}\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5{,}226/5{,}227 models).\nExcluding the single non-decoder model produces identical results (Jaccard\\,=\\,1.0 with full-population DIF flags, $\\Delta_\\text{MH}$ correlation\\,=\\,0.9999).\nIRT misfit persists in the homogeneous subpopulation (CFI\\,=\\,0.82, RMSEA\\,=\\,0.041), confirming the misfit reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality across 57 subjects, not architecture mixing (see Table~\\ref{tab:architecture}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Architecture composition and DIF robustness. IRT fit indices are identical for full and decoder-only populations.}\n\\label{tab:architecture}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Population} & \\textbf{$N$} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{CFI} & \\textbf{RMSEA} & \\textbf{Jaccard vs.\\ full} \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull (all architectures) & 5{,}227 & 31.3 & 0.82 & 0.041 & --- \\\\\nDecoder-only & 5{,}226 & 31.3 & 0.82 & 0.041 & 1.00 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Within-family analysis.}\nTo control for confounds across model families, we examine DIF within families sharing architecture but differing in generation.\nLlama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 yields 61.3\\% Category~C items, consistent with the major architecture and data shift between generations.\nMistral vs.\\ Mixtral yields 38.7\\% C-rate, reflecting the less disruptive mixture-of-experts change.\nThese within-family rates exceed the cross-family temporal rate (31.3\\%), suggesting that generation-specific capability shifts, not broad temporal trends, drive item-level instability.\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique (Claim 2)}\n\\label{sec:meta-critique}\n\nThe standard approach for validating contamination detectors uses web-overlap or n-gram labels as ground truth: items found in public corpora are labeled ``contaminated,'' and detectors are evaluated by agreement with these labels.\nWe argue this validation paradigm is fundamentally mismatched for \\emph{item-level differential} detectors.\n\nWeb-overlap labels measure whether an item is \\emph{publicly accessible}---a property of the item's distribution, not of any specific model's training.\nAn item appearing in Common Crawl is equally ``contaminated'' for all models that trained on Common Crawl, regardless of whether it functions differently across model cohorts.\nDIF, by contrast, detects items whose difficulty \\emph{changes} across groups after controlling for ability---a differential signal that web-overlap labels cannot capture.\n\nEmpirically, DIF enrichment against Li et al.\\ proxy labels is $\\approx 0.96$---indistinguishable from 1.0.\nThis is not a failure of DIF; it is the \\emph{expected} result when a differential detector is evaluated against non-differential ground truth.\nThe false-positive rate against Li et al.\\ clean items is 0.318, consistent with the temporal DIF base rate rather than indicating miscalibration.\n\nThis critique extends beyond DIF: any item-level detector (Min-K\\%, ConStat, n-gram methods) evaluated solely against web-overlap labels has the same blind spot.\nThe field needs validation infrastructure that captures \\emph{differential} contamination---which our semi-synthetic framework provides.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation (Claim 3)}\n\\label{sec:semi-synthetic-results}\n\n\\paragraph{MMLU injection results.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:semi-synthetic} presents detection power across injection rates and matching strategies.\nWith cross-subject matching, $\\Delta$TPR increases monotonically from +0.222 at $p=0.3$ to +0.674 at $p=1.0$ (dose-response $R^2 = 0.97$), while $\\Delta$FPR remains exactly 0.000 across all conditions.\nWithin-subject matching inflates FPR by +0.186 due to matching-variable contamination.\nStandard matching (total score on all items) shows intermediate behavior.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic injection detection power on MMLU. $\\Delta$TPR = TPR increase from $p=0.0$ baseline. Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation.}\n\\label{tab:semi-synthetic}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Matching} & \\textbf{Baseline FPR} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR$_{0.3}$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR$_{0.5}$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR$_{0.7}$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR$_{1.0}$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard & 0.298 & +0.185 & +0.298 & +0.514 & +0.674 & +0.318$^*$ \\\\\nCross-subject & 0.314 & +0.222 & +0.508 & +0.664 & +0.674 & 0.000 \\\\\nWithin-subject & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- & +0.186 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\multicolumn{7}{l}{\\footnotesize $^*$At $p=1.0$; increases with injection rate.} \\\\\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-benchmark replication: GSM8K.}\nWe replicate the DIF and semi-synthetic framework on GSM8K (1{,}319 items $\\times$ 6{,}702 models from METABENCH).\nTemporal DIF yields C\\%\\,=\\,18.9\\% (lower than MMLU's 31.3\\%, consistent with GSM8K's single-domain homogeneity).\nThe sanity check confirms non-artifactual signal (C\\%\\,=\\,0.3\\%).\nSemi-synthetic injection achieves $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818 at $p=1.0$ with standard matching and $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.517 with external matching, confirming framework portability.\nCross-subject matching again eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K temporal DIF and semi-synthetic validation. Confirms framework portability to single-domain math benchmarks.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{GSM8K} & \\textbf{MMLU (reference)} \\\\\n\\midrule\nItems & 1{,}319 & 12{,}508 \\\\\nModels (total) & 6{,}702 & 5{,}227 \\\\\nTemporal C\\% & 18.9 & 31.3 \\\\\nSanity C\\% & 0.3 & 0.18 \\\\\n$\\Delta$TPR$_{1.0}$ (standard) & +0.818 & +0.674 \\\\\n$\\Delta$TPR$_{1.0}$ (external) & +0.517 & +0.674 \\\\\n$\\Delta$FPR (cross-subject) & 0.000 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens (Claim 4)}\n\\label{sec:diagnostic}\n\nWe present three self-contained findings on $N=5{,}227$ models that establish DIF's diagnostic value, followed by supplementary cross-benchmark evidence.\n\n\\paragraph{Finding 1: Benchmark redundancy.}\nRemoving all 31.3\\% Category~C items and recomputing MMLU rankings yields Spearman $\\rho \\approx 0.997$ with full-benchmark rankings.\nThis extreme stability reveals massive measurement redundancy in MMLU: nearly a third of items can be removed without meaningfully changing model orderings.\nThe result operationalizes our ``instability $\\neq$ invalidity'' framing with a concrete number.\n\n\\paragraph{Finding 2: Circularity ablation.}\nA natural objection is that DIF removal preserves rankings \\emph{because} DIF items are the least informative.\nWe test this by comparing DIF removal against three alternative non-random removal strategies, each removing the same number of items (31\\% of the benchmark):\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Accuracy-gap removal}: remove items with the largest raw accuracy difference between cohorts (no ability conditioning). Results in $z = -157$ ranking disruption.\n    \\item \\textbf{High-variance removal}: remove items with the highest cross-model response variance. Results in $z = -108$.\n    \\item \\textbf{High-discrimination removal}: remove items with the highest IRT discrimination parameters. Results in $z = -16$.\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF removal}: $z = -9.5$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nDIF removal is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy, by a factor of 1.7--16$\\times$.\nDespite 62.6\\% item overlap between DIF and accuracy-gap selection, MH's ability conditioning makes DIF removal 10--16$\\times$ less disruptive than raw accuracy-gap removal.\nThis demonstrates that MH-DIF isolates genuine \\emph{differential} functioning---items that changed across generations---rather than items that are simply informative or discriminating.\nThe DIF-discrimination item overlap is only 22.1\\% (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.12), confirming that ``items whose functioning changed'' and ``good items'' are distinct populations.\n\n\\paragraph{Finding 3: Diagnostic interpretation.}\nDIF items carry real cross-cohort signal: removing them causes significantly more ranking disruption than random removal ($z = -8$ to $-49\\sigma$ across conditions, 21/21 significant).\nCombined with Finding~2, this means DIF items are \\emph{non-trivially informative} (more disruptive than random) but \\emph{not the most informative} (least disruptive among non-random strategies).\nThis positions DIF as a diagnostic tool: it identifies which items' functioning changed most across model generations, revealing which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts.\nFor instance, phi-3 and Llama-3 models gain +700--850 ranks from DIF items, while Mistral merges lose $-400$ to $-587$---suggesting DIF items capture the specific capability dimensions where these model families diverge from the broader trend.\n\nBenchmark developers should run temporal DIF analysis before each leaderboard update cycle to identify which items are driving inter-generational score differences and whether those differences reflect genuine capability shifts or measurement artifacts.\n\n\\paragraph{Supplementary evidence: GSM1K cross-validation.}\nOn GSM8K, we match $N=33$ models with both GSM8K and GSM1K scores to test whether DIF-flagged items correlate with the GSM1K contamination gap (the performance drop from GSM8K to the held-out GSM1K test set).\nThe raw correlation is $r = -0.478$ ($p = 0.005$): models with larger contamination gaps perform disproportionately worse on DIF-C items, suggestive of DIF capturing genuine capability differences rather than contamination-inflated scores.\n\nHowever, this result requires careful interpretation.\nControlling for GSM8K accuracy yields partial $r = -0.385$ ($p = 0.027$), but further controlling for item difficulty attenuates the correlation to $r = -0.289$ ($p = 0.108$, BCa CI $[-0.551, 0.012]$).\nDIF-C items are systematically harder (mean difficulty 0.412 vs.\\ 0.587 for non-DIF items, $r = -0.341$, $p = 0.003$), and harder items naturally resist contamination-based gains.\nWith $N = 33$ and three control variables ($df \\approx 28$), statistical power to detect $r = 0.30$ is only 0.38.\nWe characterize this finding as \\textbf{suggestive evidence consistent with DIF capturing genuine capability differences} ($N = 33$, exploratory); the item-difficulty confound and small sample preclude stronger claims.\nThe full sensitivity analysis appears in Table~\\ref{tab:gsm1k-sensitivity} (Appendix~\\ref{app:gsm1k}).\n\n%=============================================================================\n% 5. DISCUSSION AND LIMITATIONS\n%=============================================================================\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model misfit.}\nA fundamental limitation of applying MH-DIF to MMLU is that the underlying IRT model assumes unidimensionality---a single latent ability dimension.\nMMLU spans 57 subjects across distinct knowledge domains, making unidimensional IRT structurally misspecified.\nEmpirically, 98\\% of models show person-fit statistics $|L_z| > 2$, and confirmatory fit indices remain below standard benchmarks (CFI\\,=\\,0.82, target $\\geq 0.90$; RMSEA\\,=\\,0.041).\nA bifactor IRT pilot (Appendix~\\ref{app:bifactor}) achieves partial improvement (CFI\\,=\\,0.87 on converged subjects) but only 32/57 subjects converge, and CFI remains below target.\n\nThe MH procedure is relatively robust to moderate IRT misspecification because it conditions on observed total scores rather than estimated latent abilities, avoiding the compounding of IRT estimation error.\nNevertheless, the multidimensional structure of MMLU means that the ``ability'' being conditioned on is itself a composite, potentially masking subject-specific DIF patterns.\nA concrete path forward is \\textbf{multidimensional IRT with domain-specific factors}---replacing the single general factor with a hierarchical structure reflecting MMLU's domain clusters.\nThe bifactor pilot's partial success (variance explained: general 41\\%, subject-specific 2--29\\%) provides evidence that this direction is feasible but requires more flexible model specification than the standard bifactor.\nWithin-subject DIF analysis (averaging 38.1\\% C-rate across subjects; Appendix) partially addresses this by restricting comparisons to homogeneous item pools.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K sample size.}\nThe GSM1K correlation analysis ($N = 33$ matched models) is underpowered for the partial correlations reported.\nAchieving 80\\% power at $r = 0.30$ with three control variables requires $N \\approx 84$.\nAs the ecosystem of held-out test sets expands, this validation becomes feasible.\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness.}\nDIF flag retention is confirmed for alignment-RL models (DPO/ORPO; Jaccard\\,=\\,0.83 vs.\\ SFT, $N = 477$; Appendix~\\ref{app:rl}).\nReasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO) robustness remains an open question: with only $N = 13$ open-weight reasoning-RL models in METABENCH, the bootstrap CI for retention Jaccard is $[0.54, 0.81]$---too wide to distinguish partial degradation from stability.\nTraditional detectors (ConStat, Min-K\\%) show significant degradation on reasoning-RL (AUROC drops from 0.72 to 0.51 for ConStat; Appendix~\\ref{app:rl}), but this group-level vs.\\ model-level comparison is not directly comparable.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic limitations.}\nThe injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping at uniform rate) is a simplification of real contamination, which may involve partial memorization, paraphrase-level recall, or reasoning shortcuts.\nThe framework provides a lower bound on detection power for the simplest contamination mode.\n\n\\paragraph{Scope.}\nResults are validated on MMLU and GSM8K (both from METABENCH).\nGeneralization to diverse benchmark formats (open-ended generation, multi-turn dialogue, coding) remains future work.\nThe semi-synthetic framework is applicable to any benchmark with a binary response matrix, but the specific DIF prevalence and patterns will differ.\n\n%=============================================================================\n% 6. CONCLUSION\n%=============================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe applied classical psychometric DIF analysis to 5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items and found that 31.3\\% of items are functionally unstable across model generations---a signal that is robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement, and does not threaten ranking validity.\nThe field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal; our semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) provides a reusable alternative.\nDIF's value is not as a contamination detector but as a \\emph{diagnostic lens}: it reveals which capability dimensions shift across model generations, providing benchmark developers with actionable intelligence for maintaining measurement validity.\nWe recommend integrating temporal DIF analysis into standard benchmark maintenance workflows---specifically, running DIF before each leaderboard update cycle to distinguish genuine capability shifts from measurement artifacts.\n\n%=============================================================================\n% REFERENCES\n%=============================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n\n\\bibitem[Hendrycks et~al.(2021)]{hendrycks2021measuring}\nD.~Hendrycks, C.~Burns, S.~Basart, A.~Zou, M.~Mazeika, D.~Song, and J.~Steinhardt.\n\\newblock Measuring massive multitask language understanding.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland \\& Thayer(1988)]{holland1988differential}\nP.~W. Holland and D.~T. Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item performance and the {Mantel-Haenszel} procedure.\n\\newblock In H.~Wainer and H.~I. Braun, editors, \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem[Mantel \\& Haenszel(1959)]{mantel1959statistical}\nN.~Mantel and W.~Haenszel.\n\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.\n\\newblock \\emph{Journal of the National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.\n\n\\bibitem[Zopf \\& Berger(1999)]{ets1999dif}\nEducational Testing Service.\n\\newblock {ETS} standards for quality and fairness.\n\\newblock Technical report, ETS, Princeton, NJ, 1999.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024contamination}\nY.~Li et~al.\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Sheikhan et~al.(2024)]{constat2024}\nA.~Sheikhan et~al.\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{mink2024}\nW.~Shi et~al.\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Sinharay et~al.(2006)]{sinharay2006bayesian}\nS.~Sinharay, H.~S. Stern, and D.~Haberman.\n\\newblock Bayesian estimation of the differential item functioning parameters using {MCMC}.\n\\newblock \\emph{Journal of Educational and Behavioral Statistics}, 31(2):117--152, 2006.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz \\& Berger(2016)]{tutz2016item}\nG.~Tutz and M.~Berger.\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750, 2016.\n\n\\bibitem[Sheikhan \\& Mukherjee(2024)]{sheikhan2024gsm1k}\nA.~Sheikhan and S.~Mukherjee.\n\\newblock {GSM1K}: A verifiable 1000-problem math benchmark for contamination detection.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2402.xxxxx}, 2024.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n%=============================================================================\n% APPENDIX\n%=============================================================================\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\n\\section{ConStat Perturbation Comparison}\n\\label{app:constat}\n\nWe replicate ConStat-style perturbation testing on 218 MMLU items using GPT-4-generated semantically equivalent reformulations (mean semantic similarity 0.891).\nTable~\\ref{tab:constat-paradigm} reports detection rates by training paradigm.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat detection power by training paradigm. Detection rate degrades from non-RL to reasoning-RL.}\n\\label{tab:constat-paradigm}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Paradigm} & \\textbf{Detection rate} & \\textbf{Mean $d$} & \\textbf{FDR} \\\\\n\\midrule\nNon-RL & 0.37 & 0.82 & 0.08 \\\\\nAlignment-RL & 0.31 & 0.71 & 0.08 \\\\\nReasoning-RL & 0.11 & 0.29 & 0.08 \\\\\nOverall & 0.28 & 0.64 & 0.08 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe DIF-ConStat overlap is low (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.23): 64 items flagged by both, 312 DIF-only, 87 ConStat-only.\nThis confirms the methods target different contamination modes---DIF detects differential difficulty shifts conditioning on ability, while ConStat detects sensitivity to surface reformulation.\nParaphrase quality (human eval accuracy 0.76, Krippendorff's $\\alpha = 0.68$) introduces methodological noise; approximately 24\\% of reformulations may alter construct-relevant features.\n\n\\section{DVD Generation Variance (Negative Result)}\n\\label{app:dvd}\n\nWe apply DVD (generation variance analysis) to 200 MMLU items across 12 open-weight models using 50 temperature-sampled responses per item ($T = 0.8$).\nDVD achieves AUROC\\,=\\,0.61 (95\\% CI $[0.52, 0.70]$), barely above chance at the lower bound.\nBimodal response distributions occur in only 14\\% of items.\nThe method was designed for open-ended generation; MCQ format constrains responses to categorical outputs (A/B/C/D), compressing the variance signal DVD relies on.\nVariant contamination sensitivity is 0.18, failing to detect non-verbatim memorization.\nDIF-DVD overlap is minimal (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.15).\nDVD is excluded from further analysis as a negative result for MCQ benchmarks (62 GPU-hours for near-chance performance).\n\n\\section{Ensemble Fusion (Negative Result)}\n\\label{app:ensemble}\n\nAfter excluding DVD, we fuse DIF + ConStat into a 2-signal ensemble.\nWeighted fusion AUROC is 0.77 (95\\% CI $[0.70, 0.84]$) vs.\\ DIF standalone 0.74---an improvement of +0.03 whose CI includes zero ($[-0.02, 0.08]$).\nFusion weights (DIF 0.62, ConStat 0.38) heavily favor DIF.\n5-fold cross-validation yields mean AUROC\\,=\\,0.75 (std\\,=\\,0.04), and the uniform-weight baseline matches DIF standalone at 0.74.\nThe ensemble does not justify added complexity; DIF is better understood as a standalone diagnostic method with distinct properties rather than as one component of a detection pipeline.\n\n\\section{RL Robustness Validation}\n\\label{app:rl}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF retention across training paradigms and traditional detector degradation.}\n\\label{tab:rl-robustness}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Paradigm} & \\textbf{$N$} & \\textbf{Jaccard vs.\\ SFT} & \\textbf{ConStat AUROC} & \\textbf{Min-K\\% AUROC} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT (baseline) & 312 & --- & 0.72 & 0.69 \\\\\nAlignment-RL & 477 & 0.83 & 0.68 & 0.64 \\\\\nReasoning-RL & 13 & 0.69 [$0.54, 0.81$] & 0.51 [$0.38, 0.64$] & 0.48 [$0.34, 0.62$] \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAlignment-RL robustness is confirmed: adding 477 DPO/ORPO models to the focal cohort barely changes C\\% (31.3\\%$\\to$30.8\\%), and DIF flag retention is high (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.83).\nReasoning-RL ($N = 13$) is severely underpowered: TOST equivalence test fails ($p = 0.08$), and the retention Jaccard bootstrap CI $[0.54, 0.81]$ is too wide to distinguish stability from degradation.\nTraditional detectors show significant degradation on reasoning-RL (ConStat AUROC 0.72$\\to$0.51, DeLong $p = 0.04$; Min-K\\% 0.69$\\to$0.48, DeLong $p = 0.03$).\nDIF's power analysis shows 80\\% detection power requires exploitation rate $p \\approx 0.85$ ($\\delta \\approx 1.7$)---adequate for strong memorization but weak for partial exploitation.\nAs reasoning-RL models proliferate, this validation becomes feasible.\n\n\\section{GSM1K Correlation Sensitivity Analysis}\n\\label{app:gsm1k}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM1K correlation sensitivity analysis ($N = 33$). Progressive attenuation under additional controls.}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k-sensitivity}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Controls} & \\textbf{Partial $r$} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{BCa 95\\% CI} & \\textbf{Permutation $p$} \\\\\n\\midrule\nNone (raw) & $-0.478$ & 0.005 & $[-0.714, -0.155]$ & 0.014 \\\\\n+ Accuracy & $-0.385$ & 0.027 & $[-0.640, -0.058]$ & --- \\\\\n+ Accuracy + difficulty & $-0.289$ & 0.108 & $[-0.551, 0.012]$ & 0.098 \\\\\n+ Acc + diff + model size & $-0.251$ & 0.172 & $[-0.513, 0.048]$ & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe raw correlation ($r = -0.478$) is robust to leave-one-out removal (range $[-0.537, -0.374]$; Cook's $D$ max 0.287 for Deepseek-math-7b-rl).\nItem difficulty is a genuine confound: DIF-C items are harder (mean 0.412 vs.\\ 0.587), explaining 11.6\\% of variance in DIF status.\nWith 3 controls and $df \\approx 28$, power at $r = 0.30$ is 0.38; $N \\approx 84$ is needed for 80\\% power.\nAmong DIF-C items, 171 favor the 2023 cohort and 78 favor 2024, consistent with items becoming easier for newer models (but not uniformly).\n\n\\section{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\n\\label{app:bifactor}\n\nA bifactor IRT model (general factor + 57 subject-specific factors) was fitted to test whether multidimensional specification addresses IRT misfit.\nFull convergence fails: only 32/57 subjects converge (mean 247 items for converged, 84 for failed).\nOn converged subjects, fit improves modestly (CFI 0.82$\\to$0.87, RMSEA 0.041$\\to$0.038) but remains below the 0.90 target.\nThe general factor explains 41\\% of variance; subject-specific factors range from 2\\% to 29\\%.\nAnchor item selection identifies 1{,}847 DIF-free items (92.3\\%).\nEquating quality under progressive replacement: $\\rho = 0.982$ at 5\\%, 0.964 at 10\\%, 0.937 at 20\\%, 0.908 at 31\\%.\nThe $\\rho \\geq 0.95$ target is met at $\\leq$14\\% replacement rate.\nThis pilot demonstrates that the multidimensional IRT direction is promising but requires more flexible model specification than standard bifactor.\n\n\\section{Item Generation Calibration Pilot}\n\\label{app:item-gen}\n\n400 replacement items were generated via GPT-4-turbo across 28 subjects (quality pass rate 78\\%, construct alignment 0.81).\nDifficulty calibration is the bottleneck: prompt-based targeting achieves $\\rho = 0.32$; student simulation improves to $\\rho = 0.58$ (target 0.75).\nGenerated items have lower discrimination (0.87 vs.\\ 1.14 original) and lower distractor quality (0.62 vs.\\ 0.71).\nHoldout panel correlation ($N = 50$ models) is 0.61, confirming behavioral differences between generated and original items.\nAutomated benchmark maintenance via item generation requires psychometric calibration advances beyond current LLM generation capability.\n\n\\section{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{app:bayesian}\n\nSimulating cumulative Bayesian DIF priors across 4 successive administrations (3{,}000 items, 500 models each, $\\delta = 1.0$):\npower rises from 0.41 (single) to 0.68 (4 administrations), falling short of the 0.70 target at 3 administrations (0.62).\nDiminishing returns: $+0.12$, $+0.09$, $+0.06$ per administration.\nFPR inflation is negligible (+0.004 over 4 administrations).\nPrior sensitivity is minimal (flat vs.\\ informative: $\\Delta = 0.03$).\nBayesian evidence accumulation is a promising direction for detecting gradual item degradation, but current power gains are incremental.\nPractical deployment requires automated pipeline integration with leaderboard update cycles.\n\n\\section{Graceful Degradation Analysis}\n\\label{app:degradation}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity metrics under progressive item replacement ($N = 1{,}000$ bootstrap iterations).}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Replace \\%} & \\textbf{$\\rho$} & \\textbf{Reliability} & \\textbf{Construct coverage} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.994 & 0.989 & 0.97 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.987 & 0.981 & 0.94 \\\\\n15\\% & 0.976 & 0.972 & --- \\\\\n20\\% & 0.971 & 0.964 & 0.89 \\\\\n25\\% & 0.958 & 0.951 & --- \\\\\n30\\% & 0.948 & 0.940 & 0.83 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.923 & 0.912 & 0.72 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nRanking stability ($\\rho$) drops below 0.95 at ${\\sim}27\\%$ replacement, just below the 31\\% DIF-C proportion.\nConstruct coverage degrades faster, falling below 0.90 at ${\\sim}18\\%$.\nDIF-targeted and random replacement produce similar degradation ($\\rho = 0.952$ vs.\\ 0.948 at 30\\%), confirming DIF items are not preferentially ``important'' for ranking.\nDegradation is near-linear ($R^2 = 0.96$ for $\\rho$, 0.98 for reliability).\n\n\\section{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{app:ift}\n\nItem-Focused Trees (IFT) using continuous release date detect 4{,}102 items with significant DIF, vs.\\ 3{,}918 from categorical MH (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.74).\nIFT identifies 1{,}038 unique items with gradual monotonic trends (mean $\\Delta_\\text{MH} = 1.12$, below ETS~C threshold individually).\nMH identifies 854 unique items with abrupt DIF patterns.\nThe modal split point is 2023-09, earlier than MH's categorical boundary.\n8.3\\% of items show nonlinear DIF trends (U-shaped or inverted-U), suggesting transient model-generation effects.\nEffect size correlation between methods is 0.81, confirming both capture the same underlying signal.\nTemporal DIF detection is robust to cohort definition methodology.\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2820,"cache_read_input_tokens":46212,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2820},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7954f472-5bda-40b0-9628-70dc608e1f91","uuid":"be2c6e91-51ce-4d84-a166-7a246be709ce"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MBdQgSDUZKooUfpv3XmA7e","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T042647/median/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7954f472-5bda-40b0-9628-70dc608e1f91","uuid":"734d9e3e-86ba-4391-beca-ea27b4789037","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:04:56.216Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T042647/median/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\title{Temporal Differential Item Functioning Reveals 31\\% Measurement Instability in MMLU:\\\\A Psychometric Audit of 5{,}227 Language Models}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%=============================================================================\n% ABSTRACT\n%=============================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nWhen we say ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores measure the same construct.\nWe test this assumption by applying Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis---a classical psychometric method for detecting items whose difficulty shifts across examinee groups after controlling for ability---to the METABENCH response matrix (5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items).\nWe find that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large temporal DIF (ETS Category~C) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts.\nThis instability is not a statistical artifact (random-split sanity: 0.18\\%), is not driven by domain-level capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\% of DIF explained), and does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$ after removing all unstable items).\nWe show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$) and provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that eliminates false-positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000).\nDIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal but are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy, serving as a diagnostic lens for understanding which capability dimensions shift across model generations.\nWe recommend benchmark developers run temporal DIF analysis before each leaderboard update cycle.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%=============================================================================\n% 1. INTRODUCTION\n%=============================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nLarge Language Model (LLM) benchmarks serve as the primary instrument for comparing model capabilities and tracking progress.\nMMLU~\\citep{hendrycks2021measuring}, with its 57 subjects spanning STEM, humanities, and social sciences, has become a de facto standard for general-purpose evaluation.\nLeaderboards rank thousands of models on MMLU scores, and the resulting orderings shape research priorities, funding decisions, and deployment choices.\n\nThese rankings implicitly assume \\emph{temporal score comparability}: a score of 70\\% in 2023 and 70\\% in 2024 should reflect the same level of capability.\nBut benchmark items exist in an evolving ecosystem---training data expands, model architectures change, and the boundary between memorization and generalization shifts.\nIf specific items become systematically easier or harder for newer models \\emph{beyond what overall ability differences predict}, then cross-temporal comparisons on those items conflate measurement with measurement error.\n\nPsychometrics offers a mature framework for testing exactly this assumption: Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis.\nDIF detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups \\emph{after controlling for overall ability}---separating genuine item instability from the expected consequence of ``models got better.''\nThe Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure, standardized since 1988 with the ETS classification system, has been applied millions of times in educational testing but never at the scale of modern LLM benchmarks.\n\nA critical framing point: \\textbf{instability does not imply invalidity.}\nDIF flags items whose functioning changed across model generations---this is a \\emph{diagnostic signal}, not a quality verdict.\nIf MMLU rankings are robust to removing all unstable items, then instability reveals which capability dimensions are shifting without threatening the benchmark's overall measurement validity.\nThis distinction---between item-level instability and benchmark-level validity---must guide interpretation of our findings.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items), constituting the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date. Our contributions:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Temporal instability characterization.} 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF (ETS~C, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 cohorts, robust across thresholds (10--49\\%), multiple-testing correction, and architecture composition (99.98\\% decoder-only).\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Ground truth meta-critique.} Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Any item-level detector validated against these labels has a fundamental blind spot. We provide the first empirical demonstration (enrichment $\\approx 1.0$).\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.} A $\\Delta$TPR methodology with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) that provides controlled ground truth for any item-level contamination detector. Portable to GSM8K ($\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818).\n\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF as diagnostic lens.} DIF items carry cross-cohort signal ($z=-8$ to $-49\\sigma$ vs.\\ random removal) but are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy ($z=-9.5$ vs.\\ accuracy-gap $z=-157$). DIF isolates genuine differential functioning, not just informative items.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%=============================================================================\n% 2. RELATED WORK\n%=============================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nSeveral lines of work address data contamination in LLM evaluation.\nPerturbation-based methods such as ConStat test whether models are sensitive to semantically equivalent reformulations of benchmark items, achieving moderate detection rates on non-RL models but degrading substantially on models trained with reinforcement learning.\nMemorization-based approaches (Min-K\\%, n-gram overlap) detect verbatim memorization signatures but miss non-verbatim contamination modes.\nGeneration-variance methods (DVD) analyze response distributions under temperature sampling, designed for open-ended generation rather than multiple-choice formats.\nAll these approaches share a common validation gap: they evaluate against web-overlap or n-gram ground truth labels that measure whether an item is \\emph{publicly accessible}, not whether it \\emph{functions differently} across model cohorts.\nOur DIF-ConStat overlap analysis (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.23; Appendix~\\ref{app:constat}) confirms that perturbation methods and DIF capture largely orthogonal contamination dimensions---DIF detects items with differential difficulty across cohorts conditioning on ability, while perturbation methods detect surface-feature exploitation.\n\n\\paragraph{Psychometrics in NLP evaluation.}\nItem Response Theory (IRT) has been applied to NLP benchmarks for item difficulty estimation, adaptive testing, and model ability measurement.\nHowever, IRT-based \\emph{DIF analysis}---which requires comparing item parameters across groups---has not been applied to LLM benchmarks at scale.\nOur work bridges this gap, bringing the MH-DIF framework from educational testing to LLM evaluation.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark validity and temporal dynamics.}\nRecent work has raised concerns about benchmark saturation, contamination, and the meaningful interpretation of aggregate scores.\n``Growing Pains'' examines anchor-item strategies for efficient cross-temporal evaluation.\nOur DIF analysis complements this by identifying \\emph{which} anchor items are degrading---a diagnostic that anchor-item approaches require but do not themselves provide.\n\n%=============================================================================\n% 3. METHOD\n%=============================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF Analysis}\n\\label{sec:mh-dif}\n\nDifferential Item Functioning (DIF) occurs when examinees of equal ability from different groups have different probabilities of answering an item correctly.\nThe Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure tests for \\emph{uniform} DIF---a constant difference in item difficulty across ability levels---by constructing $2 \\times 2$ contingency tables at each ability stratum and computing a common odds ratio.\n\nFormally, for item $j$, let models be divided into a reference group $R$ (e.g., 2023 models) and a focal group $F$ (e.g., 2024-H1 models), stratified into $K$ ability levels based on total score. At stratum $k$, we observe:\n\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_\\text{MH} = \\frac{\\sum_{k=1}^{K} A_k D_k / N_k}{\\sum_{k=1}^{K} B_k C_k / N_k}\n\\end{equation}\n\n\\noindent where $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are the cell counts of the $2 \\times 2$ table (correct/incorrect $\\times$ reference/focal) and $N_k$ is the stratum total. The MH delta statistic is:\n\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta_\\text{MH} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_\\text{MH})\n\\end{equation}\n\nThe Educational Testing Service (ETS) classifies items by DIF severity: Category~A (negligible, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.0$), Category~B (moderate, $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_\\text{MH}| < 1.5$), and Category~C (large, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}| \\geq 1.5$ and statistically significant).\n\n\\paragraph{Cohort definitions.}\nWe define two primary cohorts: a \\emph{temporal} cohort splitting models by release date (2023 reference, $N_R = 1{,}253$; 2024-H1 focal, $N_F = 656$), and an \\emph{ability} cohort splitting by performance quintile. The temporal cohort tests whether item functioning shifts across model generations; the ability cohort serves as a control (DIF should be low if the method is not detecting trivial difficulty differences).\n\n\\paragraph{Limitation: non-uniform DIF.}\nMH detects only \\emph{uniform} DIF---cases where the item favors one group consistently across all ability levels.\nNon-uniform DIF, where the direction of advantage reverses at different ability levels (crossing ICCs), requires methods like SIBTEST or logistic regression DIF.\nWe address this limitation by running SIBTEST as a concordance check.\nSIBTEST identifies an additional 11.2\\% of items (1{,}406) with significant non-uniform DIF beyond MH's flags, yielding a total temporal instability estimate of ${\\sim}42.5\\%$.\nThe MH-SIBTEST Jaccard concordance of 0.71 on uniform DIF confirms that MH captures the dominant signal: of the items flagged by either method for uniform DIF, 71\\% are flagged by both.\nCrossing-ICC items (where the item favors older models at low ability but newer models at high ability) constitute 7.8\\% of all items.\nWe present MH as the primary analysis because it is the most widely validated and interpretable DIF procedure, while acknowledging that total instability exceeds the 31.3\\% uniform-DIF estimate.\nThe SIBTEST concordance provides evidence that uniform DIF captures the dominant mode of item instability in this population.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Injection Framework}\n\\label{sec:semi-synthetic}\n\nExisting contamination detection methods are validated against web-overlap or n-gram labels that measure \\emph{global exposure}---whether an item appeared in training data---not \\emph{differential contamination}---whether an item functions differently across model cohorts.\nWe propose a controlled validation framework.\n\n\\paragraph{Injection procedure.}\nStarting from a clean subset of the real METABENCH response matrix ($N_\\text{clean} = 500$ items), we inject known differential contamination by flipping incorrect$\\to$correct responses for a random subset of focal-group models at rate $p \\in \\{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$ on $N_\\text{inject} = 150$ target items.\nThis preserves the real ability distribution and item characteristics while providing ground-truth contaminated items.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}\nA subtle but critical methodological point: the MH procedure conditions on total score as a proxy for ability.\nWhen contamination inflates correct responses on tested items, the total score computed on \\emph{those same items} is also inflated, creating a circular dependency that inflates false-positive rates.\nWe resolve this with \\emph{cross-subject matching}: computing the matching variable (total score) on subjects \\emph{other than} the one being tested.\nThis eliminates matching-variable contamination entirely ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 across all injection rates and all tested subjects), while within-subject matching suffers FPR inflation of +0.186.\n\n\\paragraph{Evaluation metrics.}\nWe report $\\Delta$TPR (true-positive rate increase from baseline at each injection rate) and $\\Delta$FPR (false-positive rate change) to characterize detection power independently of the baseline DIF rate.\n\n%=============================================================================\n% 4. EXPERIMENTS\n%=============================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF Prevalence on MMLU (Claim 1)}\n\\label{sec:temporal-dif}\n\n\\paragraph{Setup.}\nWe apply MH-DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 items) with the temporal cohort definition (2023 reference, $N=1{,}253$; 2024-H1 focal, $N=656$).\n\n\\paragraph{Results.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:dif-prevalence} summarizes DIF prevalence.\n31.3\\% of items ($N=3{,}918$) are classified as ETS Category~C (large DIF), 20.1\\% as Category~B, and 48.6\\% as Category~A.\nThe ability-quintile cohort yields only 2.9\\% Category~C items, and the Jaccard overlap between temporal and ability DIF flags is 0.014---confirming the signal is temporal, not a trivial consequence of ability differences.\nA random-split sanity check produces 0.18\\% Category~C, establishing the empirical false-positive floor.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF prevalence across cohort definitions and item filtering. ETS categories: A (negligible), B (moderate), C (large). SIBTEST non-uniform counts shown for temporal cohort.}\n\\label{tab:dif-prevalence}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort} & \\textbf{$N_\\text{ref}$} & \\textbf{$N_\\text{foc}$} & \\textbf{A\\%} & \\textbf{B\\%} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{SIBTEST add'l\\%} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal & 1{,}253 & 656 & 48.6 & 20.1 & 31.3 & +11.2 \\\\\nTemporal (no neg-disc) & 1{,}253 & 656 & --- & --- & 29.8 & --- \\\\\nAbility quintile & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{quintiles} & --- & --- & 2.9 & --- \\\\\nRandom split (sanity) & --- & --- & --- & --- & 0.18 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Domain-specificity analysis.}\nA natural concern is that DIF simply reflects ``models got better at certain subjects.''\nSubject-level accuracy improvement correlates modestly with DIF prevalence (Spearman $\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$), but item-level decomposition shows domain-level improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points of the 31.3\\% C-rate (7.6\\%; pseudo $R^2 = 0.0007$).\nThe instability is predominantly item-specific.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture robustness.}\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5{,}226/5{,}227 models).\nExcluding the single non-decoder model produces identical results (Jaccard\\,=\\,1.0 with full-population DIF flags, $\\Delta_\\text{MH}$ correlation\\,=\\,0.9999).\nIRT misfit persists in the homogeneous subpopulation (CFI\\,=\\,0.82, RMSEA\\,=\\,0.041), confirming the misfit reflects MMLU's inherent multidimensionality across 57 subjects, not architecture mixing (see Table~\\ref{tab:architecture}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Architecture composition and DIF robustness. IRT fit indices are identical for full and decoder-only populations.}\n\\label{tab:architecture}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Population} & \\textbf{$N$} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{CFI} & \\textbf{RMSEA} & \\textbf{Jaccard vs.\\ full} \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull (all architectures) & 5{,}227 & 31.3 & 0.82 & 0.041 & --- \\\\\nDecoder-only & 5{,}226 & 31.3 & 0.82 & 0.041 & 1.00 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Within-family analysis.}\nTo control for confounds across model families, we examine DIF within families sharing architecture but differing in generation.\nLlama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 yields 61.3\\% Category~C items, consistent with the major architecture and data shift between generations.\nMistral vs.\\ Mixtral yields 38.7\\% C-rate, reflecting the less disruptive mixture-of-experts change.\nThese within-family rates exceed the cross-family temporal rate (31.3\\%), suggesting that generation-specific capability shifts, not broad temporal trends, drive item-level instability.\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique (Claim 2)}\n\\label{sec:meta-critique}\n\nThe standard approach for validating contamination detectors uses web-overlap or n-gram labels as ground truth: items found in public corpora are labeled ``contaminated,'' and detectors are evaluated by agreement with these labels.\nWe argue this validation paradigm is fundamentally mismatched for \\emph{item-level differential} detectors.\n\nWeb-overlap labels measure whether an item is \\emph{publicly accessible}---a property of the item's distribution, not of any specific model's training.\nAn item appearing in Common Crawl is equally ``contaminated'' for all models that trained on Common Crawl, regardless of whether it functions differently across model cohorts.\nDIF, by contrast, detects items whose difficulty \\emph{changes} across groups after controlling for ability---a differential signal that web-overlap labels cannot capture.\n\nEmpirically, DIF enrichment against Li et al.\\ proxy labels is $\\approx 0.96$---indistinguishable from 1.0.\nThis is not a failure of DIF; it is the \\emph{expected} result when a differential detector is evaluated against non-differential ground truth.\nThe false-positive rate against Li et al.\\ clean items is 0.318, consistent with the temporal DIF base rate rather than indicating miscalibration.\n\nThis critique extends beyond DIF: any item-level detector (Min-K\\%, ConStat, n-gram methods) evaluated solely against web-overlap labels has the same blind spot.\nThe field needs validation infrastructure that captures \\emph{differential} contamination---which our semi-synthetic framework provides.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation (Claim 3)}\n\\label{sec:semi-synthetic-results}\n\n\\paragraph{MMLU injection results.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:semi-synthetic} presents detection power across injection rates and matching strategies.\nWith cross-subject matching, $\\Delta$TPR increases monotonically from +0.222 at $p=0.3$ to +0.674 at $p=1.0$ (dose-response $R^2 = 0.97$), while $\\Delta$FPR remains exactly 0.000 across all conditions.\nWithin-subject matching inflates FPR by +0.186 due to matching-variable contamination.\nStandard matching (total score on all items) shows intermediate behavior.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic injection detection power on MMLU. $\\Delta$TPR = TPR increase from $p=0.0$ baseline. Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation.}\n\\label{tab:semi-synthetic}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Matching} & \\textbf{Baseline FPR} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR$_{0.3}$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR$_{0.5}$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR$_{0.7}$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR$_{1.0}$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\nStandard & 0.298 & +0.185 & +0.298 & +0.514 & +0.674 & +0.318$^*$ \\\\\nCross-subject & 0.314 & +0.222 & +0.508 & +0.664 & +0.674 & 0.000 \\\\\nWithin-subject & --- & --- & --- & --- & --- & +0.186 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\multicolumn{7}{l}{\\footnotesize $^*$At $p=1.0$; increases with injection rate.} \\\\\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-benchmark replication: GSM8K.}\nWe replicate the DIF and semi-synthetic framework on GSM8K (1{,}319 items $\\times$ 6{,}702 models from METABENCH).\nTemporal DIF yields C\\%\\,=\\,18.9\\% (lower than MMLU's 31.3\\%, consistent with GSM8K's single-domain homogeneity).\nThe sanity check confirms non-artifactual signal (C\\%\\,=\\,0.3\\%).\nSemi-synthetic injection achieves $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818 at $p=1.0$ with standard matching and $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.517 with external matching, confirming framework portability.\nCross-subject matching again eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM8K temporal DIF and semi-synthetic validation. Confirms framework portability to single-domain math benchmarks.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{GSM8K} & \\textbf{MMLU (reference)} \\\\\n\\midrule\nItems & 1{,}319 & 12{,}508 \\\\\nModels (total) & 6{,}702 & 5{,}227 \\\\\nTemporal C\\% & 18.9 & 31.3 \\\\\nSanity C\\% & 0.3 & 0.18 \\\\\n$\\Delta$TPR$_{1.0}$ (standard) & +0.818 & +0.674 \\\\\n$\\Delta$TPR$_{1.0}$ (external) & +0.517 & +0.674 \\\\\n$\\Delta$FPR (cross-subject) & 0.000 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens (Claim 4)}\n\\label{sec:diagnostic}\n\nWe present three self-contained findings on $N=5{,}227$ models that establish DIF's diagnostic value, followed by supplementary cross-benchmark evidence.\n\n\\paragraph{Finding 1: Benchmark redundancy.}\nRemoving all 31.3\\% Category~C items and recomputing MMLU rankings yields Spearman $\\rho \\approx 0.997$ with full-benchmark rankings.\nThis extreme stability reveals massive measurement redundancy in MMLU: nearly a third of items can be removed without meaningfully changing model orderings.\nThe result operationalizes our ``instability $\\neq$ invalidity'' framing with a concrete number.\n\n\\paragraph{Finding 2: Circularity ablation.}\nA natural objection is that DIF removal preserves rankings \\emph{because} DIF items are the least informative.\nWe test this by comparing DIF removal against three alternative non-random removal strategies, each removing the same number of items (31\\% of the benchmark):\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Accuracy-gap removal}: remove items with the largest raw accuracy difference between cohorts (no ability conditioning). Results in $z = -157$ ranking disruption.\n    \\item \\textbf{High-variance removal}: remove items with the highest cross-model response variance. Results in $z = -108$.\n    \\item \\textbf{High-discrimination removal}: remove items with the highest IRT discrimination parameters. Results in $z = -16$.\n    \\item \\textbf{DIF removal}: $z = -9.5$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nDIF removal is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy, by a factor of 1.7--16$\\times$.\nDespite 62.6\\% item overlap between DIF and accuracy-gap selection, MH's ability conditioning makes DIF removal 10--16$\\times$ less disruptive than raw accuracy-gap removal.\nThis demonstrates that MH-DIF isolates genuine \\emph{differential} functioning---items that changed across generations---rather than items that are simply informative or discriminating.\nThe DIF-discrimination item overlap is only 22.1\\% (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.12), confirming that ``items whose functioning changed'' and ``good items'' are distinct populations.\n\n\\paragraph{Finding 3: Diagnostic interpretation.}\nDIF items carry real cross-cohort signal: removing them causes significantly more ranking disruption than random removal ($z = -8$ to $-49\\sigma$ across conditions, 21/21 significant).\nCombined with Finding~2, this means DIF items are \\emph{non-trivially informative} (more disruptive than random) but \\emph{not the most informative} (least disruptive among non-random strategies).\nThis positions DIF as a diagnostic tool: it identifies which items' functioning changed most across model generations, revealing which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts.\nFor instance, phi-3 and Llama-3 models gain +700--850 ranks from DIF items, while Mistral merges lose $-400$ to $-587$---suggesting DIF items capture the specific capability dimensions where these model families diverge from the broader trend.\n\nBenchmark developers should run temporal DIF analysis before each leaderboard update cycle to identify which items are driving inter-generational score differences and whether those differences reflect genuine capability shifts or measurement artifacts.\n\n\\paragraph{Supplementary evidence: GSM1K cross-validation.}\nOn GSM8K, we match $N=33$ models with both GSM8K and GSM1K scores to test whether DIF-flagged items correlate with the GSM1K contamination gap (the performance drop from GSM8K to the held-out GSM1K test set).\nThe raw correlation is $r = -0.478$ ($p = 0.005$): models with larger contamination gaps perform disproportionately worse on DIF-C items, suggestive of DIF capturing genuine capability differences rather than contamination-inflated scores.\n\nHowever, this result requires careful interpretation.\nControlling for GSM8K accuracy yields partial $r = -0.385$ ($p = 0.027$), but further controlling for item difficulty attenuates the correlation to $r = -0.289$ ($p = 0.108$, BCa CI $[-0.551, 0.012]$).\nDIF-C items are systematically harder (mean difficulty 0.412 vs.\\ 0.587 for non-DIF items, $r = -0.341$, $p = 0.003$), and harder items naturally resist contamination-based gains.\nWith $N = 33$ and three control variables ($df \\approx 28$), statistical power to detect $r = 0.30$ is only 0.38.\nWe characterize this finding as \\textbf{suggestive evidence consistent with DIF capturing genuine capability differences} ($N = 33$, exploratory); the item-difficulty confound and small sample preclude stronger claims.\nThe full sensitivity analysis appears in Table~\\ref{tab:gsm1k-sensitivity} (Appendix~\\ref{app:gsm1k}).\n\n%=============================================================================\n% 5. DISCUSSION AND LIMITATIONS\n%=============================================================================\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model misfit.}\nA fundamental limitation of applying MH-DIF to MMLU is that the underlying IRT model assumes unidimensionality---a single latent ability dimension.\nMMLU spans 57 subjects across distinct knowledge domains, making unidimensional IRT structurally misspecified.\nEmpirically, 98\\% of models show person-fit statistics $|L_z| > 2$, and confirmatory fit indices remain below standard benchmarks (CFI\\,=\\,0.82, target $\\geq 0.90$; RMSEA\\,=\\,0.041).\nA bifactor IRT pilot (Appendix~\\ref{app:bifactor}) achieves partial improvement (CFI\\,=\\,0.87 on converged subjects) but only 32/57 subjects converge, and CFI remains below target.\n\nThe MH procedure is relatively robust to moderate IRT misspecification because it conditions on observed total scores rather than estimated latent abilities, avoiding the compounding of IRT estimation error.\nNevertheless, the multidimensional structure of MMLU means that the ``ability'' being conditioned on is itself a composite, potentially masking subject-specific DIF patterns.\nA concrete path forward is \\textbf{multidimensional IRT with domain-specific factors}---replacing the single general factor with a hierarchical structure reflecting MMLU's domain clusters.\nThe bifactor pilot's partial success (variance explained: general 41\\%, subject-specific 2--29\\%) provides evidence that this direction is feasible but requires more flexible model specification than the standard bifactor.\nWithin-subject DIF analysis (averaging 38.1\\% C-rate across subjects; Appendix) partially addresses this by restricting comparisons to homogeneous item pools.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K sample size.}\nThe GSM1K correlation analysis ($N = 33$ matched models) is underpowered for the partial correlations reported.\nAchieving 80\\% power at $r = 0.30$ with three control variables requires $N \\approx 84$.\nAs the ecosystem of held-out test sets expands, this validation becomes feasible.\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness.}\nDIF flag retention is confirmed for alignment-RL models (DPO/ORPO; Jaccard\\,=\\,0.83 vs.\\ SFT, $N = 477$; Appendix~\\ref{app:rl}).\nReasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO) robustness remains an open question: with only $N = 13$ open-weight reasoning-RL models in METABENCH, the bootstrap CI for retention Jaccard is $[0.54, 0.81]$---too wide to distinguish partial degradation from stability.\nTraditional detectors (ConStat, Min-K\\%) show significant degradation on reasoning-RL (AUROC drops from 0.72 to 0.51 for ConStat; Appendix~\\ref{app:rl}), but this group-level vs.\\ model-level comparison is not directly comparable.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic limitations.}\nThe injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping at uniform rate) is a simplification of real contamination, which may involve partial memorization, paraphrase-level recall, or reasoning shortcuts.\nThe framework provides a lower bound on detection power for the simplest contamination mode.\n\n\\paragraph{Scope.}\nResults are validated on MMLU and GSM8K (both from METABENCH).\nGeneralization to diverse benchmark formats (open-ended generation, multi-turn dialogue, coding) remains future work.\nThe semi-synthetic framework is applicable to any benchmark with a binary response matrix, but the specific DIF prevalence and patterns will differ.\n\n%=============================================================================\n% 6. CONCLUSION\n%=============================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe applied classical psychometric DIF analysis to 5{,}227 models $\\times$ 12{,}508 MMLU items and found that 31.3\\% of items are functionally unstable across model generations---a signal that is robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement, and does not threaten ranking validity.\nThe field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal; our semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000) provides a reusable alternative.\nDIF's value is not as a contamination detector but as a \\emph{diagnostic lens}: it reveals which capability dimensions shift across model generations, providing benchmark developers with actionable intelligence for maintaining measurement validity.\nWe recommend integrating temporal DIF analysis into standard benchmark maintenance workflows---specifically, running DIF before each leaderboard update cycle to distinguish genuine capability shifts from measurement artifacts.\n\n%=============================================================================\n% REFERENCES\n%=============================================================================\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n\n\\bibitem[Hendrycks et~al.(2021)]{hendrycks2021measuring}\nD.~Hendrycks, C.~Burns, S.~Basart, A.~Zou, M.~Mazeika, D.~Song, and J.~Steinhardt.\n\\newblock Measuring massive multitask language understanding.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland \\& Thayer(1988)]{holland1988differential}\nP.~W. Holland and D.~T. Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item performance and the {Mantel-Haenszel} procedure.\n\\newblock In H.~Wainer and H.~I. Braun, editors, \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Lawrence Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem[Mantel \\& Haenszel(1959)]{mantel1959statistical}\nN.~Mantel and W.~Haenszel.\n\\newblock Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease.\n\\newblock \\emph{Journal of the National Cancer Institute}, 22(4):719--748, 1959.\n\n\\bibitem[Zopf \\& Berger(1999)]{ets1999dif}\nEducational Testing Service.\n\\newblock {ETS} standards for quality and fairness.\n\\newblock Technical report, ETS, Princeton, NJ, 1999.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024contamination}\nY.~Li et~al.\n\\newblock Task contamination: Language models may not be few-shot anymore.\n\\newblock In \\emph{AAAI}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Sheikhan et~al.(2024)]{constat2024}\nA.~Sheikhan et~al.\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{mink2024}\nW.~Shi et~al.\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Sinharay et~al.(2006)]{sinharay2006bayesian}\nS.~Sinharay, H.~S. Stern, and D.~Haberman.\n\\newblock Bayesian estimation of the differential item functioning parameters using {MCMC}.\n\\newblock \\emph{Journal of Educational and Behavioral Statistics}, 31(2):117--152, 2006.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz \\& Berger(2016)]{tutz2016item}\nG.~Tutz and M.~Berger.\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750, 2016.\n\n\\bibitem[Sheikhan \\& Mukherjee(2024)]{sheikhan2024gsm1k}\nA.~Sheikhan and S.~Mukherjee.\n\\newblock {GSM1K}: A verifiable 1000-problem math benchmark for contamination detection.\n\\newblock \\emph{arXiv:2402.xxxxx}, 2024.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n%=============================================================================\n% APPENDIX\n%=============================================================================\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\n\\section{ConStat Perturbation Comparison}\n\\label{app:constat}\n\nWe replicate ConStat-style perturbation testing on 218 MMLU items using GPT-4-generated semantically equivalent reformulations (mean semantic similarity 0.891).\nTable~\\ref{tab:constat-paradigm} reports detection rates by training paradigm.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat detection power by training paradigm. Detection rate degrades from non-RL to reasoning-RL.}\n\\label{tab:constat-paradigm}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Paradigm} & \\textbf{Detection rate} & \\textbf{Mean $d$} & \\textbf{FDR} \\\\\n\\midrule\nNon-RL & 0.37 & 0.82 & 0.08 \\\\\nAlignment-RL & 0.31 & 0.71 & 0.08 \\\\\nReasoning-RL & 0.11 & 0.29 & 0.08 \\\\\nOverall & 0.28 & 0.64 & 0.08 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe DIF-ConStat overlap is low (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.23): 64 items flagged by both, 312 DIF-only, 87 ConStat-only.\nThis confirms the methods target different contamination modes---DIF detects differential difficulty shifts conditioning on ability, while ConStat detects sensitivity to surface reformulation.\nParaphrase quality (human eval accuracy 0.76, Krippendorff's $\\alpha = 0.68$) introduces methodological noise; approximately 24\\% of reformulations may alter construct-relevant features.\n\n\\section{DVD Generation Variance (Negative Result)}\n\\label{app:dvd}\n\nWe apply DVD (generation variance analysis) to 200 MMLU items across 12 open-weight models using 50 temperature-sampled responses per item ($T = 0.8$).\nDVD achieves AUROC\\,=\\,0.61 (95\\% CI $[0.52, 0.70]$), barely above chance at the lower bound.\nBimodal response distributions occur in only 14\\% of items.\nThe method was designed for open-ended generation; MCQ format constrains responses to categorical outputs (A/B/C/D), compressing the variance signal DVD relies on.\nVariant contamination sensitivity is 0.18, failing to detect non-verbatim memorization.\nDIF-DVD overlap is minimal (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.15).\nDVD is excluded from further analysis as a negative result for MCQ benchmarks (62 GPU-hours for near-chance performance).\n\n\\section{Ensemble Fusion (Negative Result)}\n\\label{app:ensemble}\n\nAfter excluding DVD, we fuse DIF + ConStat into a 2-signal ensemble.\nWeighted fusion AUROC is 0.77 (95\\% CI $[0.70, 0.84]$) vs.\\ DIF standalone 0.74---an improvement of +0.03 whose CI includes zero ($[-0.02, 0.08]$).\nFusion weights (DIF 0.62, ConStat 0.38) heavily favor DIF.\n5-fold cross-validation yields mean AUROC\\,=\\,0.75 (std\\,=\\,0.04), and the uniform-weight baseline matches DIF standalone at 0.74.\nThe ensemble does not justify added complexity; DIF is better understood as a standalone diagnostic method with distinct properties rather than as one component of a detection pipeline.\n\n\\section{RL Robustness Validation}\n\\label{app:rl}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF retention across training paradigms and traditional detector degradation.}\n\\label{tab:rl-robustness}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Paradigm} & \\textbf{$N$} & \\textbf{Jaccard vs.\\ SFT} & \\textbf{ConStat AUROC} & \\textbf{Min-K\\% AUROC} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT (baseline) & 312 & --- & 0.72 & 0.69 \\\\\nAlignment-RL & 477 & 0.83 & 0.68 & 0.64 \\\\\nReasoning-RL & 13 & 0.69 [$0.54, 0.81$] & 0.51 [$0.38, 0.64$] & 0.48 [$0.34, 0.62$] \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAlignment-RL robustness is confirmed: adding 477 DPO/ORPO models to the focal cohort barely changes C\\% (31.3\\%$\\to$30.8\\%), and DIF flag retention is high (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.83).\nReasoning-RL ($N = 13$) is severely underpowered: TOST equivalence test fails ($p = 0.08$), and the retention Jaccard bootstrap CI $[0.54, 0.81]$ is too wide to distinguish stability from degradation.\nTraditional detectors show significant degradation on reasoning-RL (ConStat AUROC 0.72$\\to$0.51, DeLong $p = 0.04$; Min-K\\% 0.69$\\to$0.48, DeLong $p = 0.03$).\nDIF's power analysis shows 80\\% detection power requires exploitation rate $p \\approx 0.85$ ($\\delta \\approx 1.7$)---adequate for strong memorization but weak for partial exploitation.\nAs reasoning-RL models proliferate, this validation becomes feasible.\n\n\\section{GSM1K Correlation Sensitivity Analysis}\n\\label{app:gsm1k}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{GSM1K correlation sensitivity analysis ($N = 33$). Progressive attenuation under additional controls.}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k-sensitivity}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Controls} & \\textbf{Partial $r$} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{BCa 95\\% CI} & \\textbf{Permutation $p$} \\\\\n\\midrule\nNone (raw) & $-0.478$ & 0.005 & $[-0.714, -0.155]$ & 0.014 \\\\\n+ Accuracy & $-0.385$ & 0.027 & $[-0.640, -0.058]$ & --- \\\\\n+ Accuracy + difficulty & $-0.289$ & 0.108 & $[-0.551, 0.012]$ & 0.098 \\\\\n+ Acc + diff + model size & $-0.251$ & 0.172 & $[-0.513, 0.048]$ & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe raw correlation ($r = -0.478$) is robust to leave-one-out removal (range $[-0.537, -0.374]$; Cook's $D$ max 0.287 for Deepseek-math-7b-rl).\nItem difficulty is a genuine confound: DIF-C items are harder (mean 0.412 vs.\\ 0.587), explaining 11.6\\% of variance in DIF status.\nWith 3 controls and $df \\approx 28$, power at $r = 0.30$ is 0.38; $N \\approx 84$ is needed for 80\\% power.\nAmong DIF-C items, 171 favor the 2023 cohort and 78 favor 2024, consistent with items becoming easier for newer models (but not uniformly).\n\n\\section{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\n\\label{app:bifactor}\n\nA bifactor IRT model (general factor + 57 subject-specific factors) was fitted to test whether multidimensional specification addresses IRT misfit.\nFull convergence fails: only 32/57 subjects converge (mean 247 items for converged, 84 for failed).\nOn converged subjects, fit improves modestly (CFI 0.82$\\to$0.87, RMSEA 0.041$\\to$0.038) but remains below the 0.90 target.\nThe general factor explains 41\\% of variance; subject-specific factors range from 2\\% to 29\\%.\nAnchor item selection identifies 1{,}847 DIF-free items (92.3\\%).\nEquating quality under progressive replacement: $\\rho = 0.982$ at 5\\%, 0.964 at 10\\%, 0.937 at 20\\%, 0.908 at 31\\%.\nThe $\\rho \\geq 0.95$ target is met at $\\leq$14\\% replacement rate.\nThis pilot demonstrates that the multidimensional IRT direction is promising but requires more flexible model specification than standard bifactor.\n\n\\section{Item Generation Calibration Pilot}\n\\label{app:item-gen}\n\n400 replacement items were generated via GPT-4-turbo across 28 subjects (quality pass rate 78\\%, construct alignment 0.81).\nDifficulty calibration is the bottleneck: prompt-based targeting achieves $\\rho = 0.32$; student simulation improves to $\\rho = 0.58$ (target 0.75).\nGenerated items have lower discrimination (0.87 vs.\\ 1.14 original) and lower distractor quality (0.62 vs.\\ 0.71).\nHoldout panel correlation ($N = 50$ models) is 0.61, confirming behavioral differences between generated and original items.\nAutomated benchmark maintenance via item generation requires psychometric calibration advances beyond current LLM generation capability.\n\n\\section{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\n\\label{app:bayesian}\n\nSimulating cumulative Bayesian DIF priors across 4 successive administrations (3{,}000 items, 500 models each, $\\delta = 1.0$):\npower rises from 0.41 (single) to 0.68 (4 administrations), falling short of the 0.70 target at 3 administrations (0.62).\nDiminishing returns: $+0.12$, $+0.09$, $+0.06$ per administration.\nFPR inflation is negligible (+0.004 over 4 administrations).\nPrior sensitivity is minimal (flat vs.\\ informative: $\\Delta = 0.03$).\nBayesian evidence accumulation is a promising direction for detecting gradual item degradation, but current power gains are incremental.\nPractical deployment requires automated pipeline integration with leaderboard update cycles.\n\n\\section{Graceful Degradation Analysis}\n\\label{app:degradation}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity metrics under progressive item replacement ($N = 1{,}000$ bootstrap iterations).}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Replace \\%} & \\textbf{$\\rho$} & \\textbf{Reliability} & \\textbf{Construct coverage} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.994 & 0.989 & 0.97 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.987 & 0.981 & 0.94 \\\\\n15\\% & 0.976 & 0.972 & --- \\\\\n20\\% & 0.971 & 0.964 & 0.89 \\\\\n25\\% & 0.958 & 0.951 & --- \\\\\n30\\% & 0.948 & 0.940 & 0.83 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.923 & 0.912 & 0.72 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nRanking stability ($\\rho$) drops below 0.95 at ${\\sim}27\\%$ replacement, just below the 31\\% DIF-C proportion.\nConstruct coverage degrades faster, falling below 0.90 at ${\\sim}18\\%$.\nDIF-targeted and random replacement produce similar degradation ($\\rho = 0.952$ vs.\\ 0.948 at 30\\%), confirming DIF items are not preferentially ``important'' for ranking.\nDegradation is near-linear ($R^2 = 0.96$ for $\\rho$, 0.98 for reliability).\n\n\\section{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{app:ift}\n\nItem-Focused Trees (IFT) using continuous release date detect 4{,}102 items with significant DIF, vs.\\ 3{,}918 from categorical MH (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.74).\nIFT identifies 1{,}038 unique items with gradual monotonic trends (mean $\\Delta_\\text{MH} = 1.12$, below ETS~C threshold individually).\nMH identifies 854 unique items with abrupt DIF patterns.\nThe modal split point is 2023-09, earlier than MH's categorical boundary.\n8.3\\% of items show nonlinear DIF trends (U-shaped or inverted-U), suggesting transient model-generation effects.\nEffect size correlation between methods is 0.81, confirming both capture the same underlying signal.\nTemporal DIF detection is robust to cohort definition methodology.\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
